window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-29"]=[{"id":"q4-10","q":"4:10","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:10 — The grave sin of consuming an orphan’s estate and the abuse of spiritual authority","source":"Cairo Arabic edition I:470–471","cairo":"I:470–471","source_page_first":470,"source_page_last":471,"ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ يَأْكُلُونَ أَمْوالَ الْيَتامى ظُلْماً إِنَّما يَأْكُلُونَ فِي بُطُونِهِمْ نارًا وَسَيَصْلَوْنَ سَعِيراً (10)\nقلت: (ظلمًا) : تمييز، أو مفعول لأجله.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّ الَّذِينَ يَأْكُلُونَ أَمْوالَ الْيَتامى ظُلْماً من غير موجب شرعي، إِنَّما يَأْكُلُونَ فِي بُطُونِهِمْ نارًا، أي: ما يجُر إلى النار ويؤول إليها.\nوعن أبي برزة أنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم قال: «يبعثُ اللهُ أقوامًا من قبورهم تتأججُ أفواههم نارًا» ، فقيل: مَنْ هم يا رسول الله؟\nقال: «ألم تر أن الله يقول: إِنَّ الَّذِينَ يَأْكُلُونَ أَمْوالَ الْيَتامى ظُلْماً إِنَّما يَأْكُلُونَ فِي بُطُونِهِمْ نارًا وَسَيَصْلَوْنَ سَعِيراً» .\nأي: يحترقون في نار، وأي نار!! والصَّلْى: هو الشيّ، تقول: صليت الشيء: شويته، وأصليته وصليته، وذكر البطون مبالغة وتهجين لحالهم.\nالإشارة: حذَّر الحق- جل جلاله- أهل الدعوى، الذين نصبوا أنفسهم للشيخوخة، وادعوا مقام التربية، مع كونهم جهالاً بالله، محجوبين عن شهود أسرار التوحيد، أن يأخذوا أموال الضعفاء الذين تعلقوا بهم لأنهم إنما يدفعون لهم ذلك طمعًا في الوصول إلى الله. وهم ليسوا أهلاً لذلك، فإذا أكلوا ذلك فإنما يأكلون في بطونهم نارًا وسيصلون سعيرًا، وهو تكثيف الحجاب، وزيادة العنت والتعب، إن أقبل عليهم الناس فرحوا واستبشروا، وإن أدبروا عنهم حزنوا وغضبوا، فأيُّ عذاب أعظم مِنْ هذا!! فتحصَّل من أول الآية إلى آخرها، أن الحق- تعالى- أمر أهل الغنى الأكبر، وهم الذين أهلَّهم للتربية النبوية، بأن سلكوا الطريق وأشرقت عليهم شموس التحقيق على يد شيخ كامل، بالاستعفاف، ولا يأخذوا إلاّ قدر الحاجة، من أموال مّنْ انتسب إليهم، وسد الباب لأهل الدعوى، لأنه مِنْ أكْلِ أموال الناس بالباطل، لأنه يعطى على وجه لم يوجد في المعطى إليه، إلا إذا كان على وجه الصدقة المحضة، مع أنه قد يكون غير مستحق لها. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم بيَّن الحقّ تعالى قسمة التركة، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ يَأْكُلُونَ أَمْوالَ الْيَتامى ظُلْماً إِنَّما يَأْكُلُونَ فِي بُطُونِهِمْ نارًا وَسَيَصْلَوْنَ سَعِيراً (10)","quran_en":"10. Those who wrongfully consume the property of orphans consume nothing but fire into their bellies, and they will enter a blazing Fire.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The accusative ẓulman (ظلمًا)","html":"<p>I say: “Wrongfully” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ظلمًا<\/bdi>) is an accusative of specification (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تمييز<\/bdi>) or an accusative of purpose or cause (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول لأجله<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Wrongfully” ( ظلمًا ) is an accusative of specification ( تمييز ) or an accusative of purpose or cause ( مفعول لأجله )."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The fire concealed in wrongful consumption","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His greatness—says: “Those who consume the wealth of orphans wrongfully,” without any lawful entitlement, “consume only fire within their bellies,” meaning what inevitably draws them toward the Fire and leads to it. Abū Barza relates that the Prophet said: “God will raise a people from their graves with fire blazing from their mouths.” Asked, “Who are they, Messenger of God?” he replied: “Have you not seen that God says, ‘Those who wrongfully consume the wealth of orphans consume only fire within their bellies, and shall burn in a Blaze’?” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H009\">[H009]<\/a><\/p><p>That is, they will burn in fire—and what a fire! <i>Ṣaly<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصَّلْي<\/bdi>) is roasting. One says “I roasted the thing” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صليت الشيء<\/bdi>), and the causative forms are <i>aṣlaytuhu<\/i> and <i>ṣallaytuhu<\/i>. Mentioning the bellies heightens the depiction and makes their conduct seem all the more repellent. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N010\">[N010]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His greatness—says: “Those who consume the wealth of orphans wrongfully,” without any lawful entitlement, “consume only fire within their bellies,” meaning what inevitably draws them toward the Fire and leads to it. Abū Barza relates that the Prophet said: “God will raise a people from their graves with fire blazing from their mouths.” Asked, “Who are they, Messenger of God?” he replied: “Have you not seen that God says, ‘Those who wrongfully consume the wealth of orphans consume only fire within their bellies, and shall burn in a Blaze’?” [H009] That is, they will burn in fire—and what a fire! Ṣaly ( الصَّلْي ) is roasting. One says “I roasted the thing” ( صليت الشيء ), and the causative forms are aṣlaytuhu and ṣallaytuhu . Mentioning the bellies heightens the depiction and makes their conduct seem all the more repellent. [N010]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"False claims to teaching and the exploitation of disciples","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His greatness—warns those who make false claims (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل الدعوى<\/bdi>), who establish themselves as shaykhs and claim the station of spiritual education while remaining ignorant of God and veiled from witnessing the secrets of divine unity, not to accept wealth from the weak who have attached themselves to them. Such people give their property in the hope of being brought to God. Those false teachers are unqualified for what they claim; in taking the offerings, they consume only fire in their bellies, and their end is a blazing flame—here, a thickening of the veil (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تكثيف الحجاب<\/bdi>) and an increase in painful labor and distress. When people turn toward them, they exult; when people turn away, they grieve and rage. What torment could exceed this?<\/p><p>The sense of the discussion from the beginning of this group of verses to its close is thus that God commands the people of the greater wealth—those truly fitted for prophetic education because they have travelled the way and the suns of verification (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شموس التحقيق<\/bdi>) have shone upon them through a perfected teacher—to abstain from taking from the wealth of those who attach themselves to them, except to the measure of need. He closes the door to pretenders, for their reception of offerings amounts to consuming other people's property falsely: the gift is offered in exchange for a spiritual reality that the supposed recipient does not possess. The exception is a gift made as purely voluntary alms (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صدقة محضة<\/bdi>), although even then the recipient may not be worthy of receiving alms. God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The Real—majestic is His greatness—warns those who make false claims ( أهل الدعوى ), who establish themselves as shaykhs and claim the station of spiritual education while remaining ignorant of God and veiled from witnessing the secrets of divine unity, not to accept wealth from the weak who have attached themselves to them. Such people give their property in the hope of being brought to God. Those false teachers are unqualified for what they claim; in taking the offerings, they consume only fire in their bellies, and their end is a blazing flame—here, a thickening of the veil ( تكثيف الحجاب ) and an increase in painful labor and distress. When people turn toward them, they exult; when people turn away, they grieve and rage. What torment could exceed this? The sense of the discussion from the beginning of this group of verses to its close is thus that God commands the people of the greater wealth—those truly fitted for prophetic education because they have travelled the way and the suns of verification ( شموس التحقيق ) have shone upon them through a perfected teacher—to abstain from taking from the wealth of those who attach themselves to them, except to the measure of need. He closes the door to pretenders, for their reception of offerings amounts to consuming other people's property falsely: the gift is offered in exchange for a spiritual reality that the supposed recipient does not possess. The exception is a gift made as purely voluntary alms ( صدقة محضة ), although even then the recipient may not be worthy of receiving alms. God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then set forth the division of inheritance.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then set forth the division of inheritance."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2606,"arabic_characters":1649},{"id":"q4-11","q":"4:11","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:11 — The portions of children and parents; grammar, inheritance and spiritual giving","source":"Cairo Arabic edition I:471–475","cairo":"I:471–475","source_page_first":471,"source_page_last":475,"ar":"يُوصِيكُمُ اللَّهُ فِي أَوْلادِكُمْ لِلذَّكَرِ مِثْلُ حَظِّ الْأُنْثَيَيْنِ ...\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: (يوصيكم الله) أي: يأمركم ويعهد إليكم، فِي أَوْلادِكُمْ، أي: في بيان ميراثهم، ثم فصَّله فقال: لِلذَّكَرِ مِثْلُ حَظِّ الْأُنْثَيَيْنِ، أي يعُدُ كل ذكر بأنثيين، فإذا ترك ابنًا وبنتًا، كانت من ثلاثة، للذكر سهمان وللبنت سهم، وإذا ترك ابنًا وبنتين فله قسمتان، ولكل واحدة قسمة، وهكذا، قال ابن جزي: هذه الآية نزلت بسبب سعد بن الربيع، وقيل: بسبب جابر بن عبد الله، إذ عاده رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم في مرضه ورفَعت ما كان في الجاهلية من ترك توريث النساء والأطفال. وقيل: نُسخت الوصية للوالدين والأقربين.\nوإنما قال: «يوصيكم» بلفظ الفعل الدائم، ولم يقل: أوصاكم، تنبيهًا على نسخ ما مضى، والشروع في حكم آخر، وإنما قال: (يوصيكم) بالاسم الظاهر، أي: (الله) ولم يقل: نوصيكم، لأنه أراد تعظيم الوصية، فجاء بالاسم الذي هو أعظم الأسماء، وإنما قال: (في أولادكم) ولم يقل: في أبنائكم لأن الابن يقع على الابن من الرضاعة، وعلى ابن البنت، وعلى الابن المتبنى، وليسوا من الورثة، فإن قيل: هلاّ قال: للأنثيين مثل حظ الذَّكر، أو للأنثى\nنصف حظ الذَّكر؟، فالجواب، أنه بدأ بالذَّكَرِ لفضله، ولأن القصد ذكُر حظه، ولو قال للأنثيين مثل حظ الذكر لكان فيه تفضيل للإناث. هـ (١) .\nالإشارة: كما أوصى الله- تعالى- في أولاد البشرية، أوصى على أولاد الروحانية، ويقع التفضيل في قسمة الإمداد على حسب التعظيم والمحبة والعطف من الشيخ، فبقدر ما يقع في قلب الشيخ، يسري إليه المدد، فقد يأخذ مثل حظ رجلين أو أكثر، على حسب ما سبق في القسمة الأزلية. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر حكم البنات إذا انفردن، فقال:\n\n... فَإِنْ كُنَّ نِساءً فَوْقَ اثْنَتَيْنِ فَلَهُنَّ ثُلُثا مَا تَرَكَ وَإِنْ كانَتْ واحِدَةً فَلَهَا النِّصْفُ ...\nقلت: أنّث الضمير فى (كنّ) باعتبار الخبر، أو يعود على المتروكات، وما قاله الزمخشري بعيد. ومن قرأ (واحدةُ) بالرفع، ففاعِلُ كَانَ التامة، ومن قرأ بالنصب، فخبر كان.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: فإن كان المتروك من الأولاد نِساءً ليس معهن ذكور فَوْقَ اثْنَتَيْنِ أي:\nاثنتين فما فوق، فَلَهُنَّ ثُلُثا ما تَرَكَ، والباقي للعاصب، وأخذ ابن عباس بظاهر الآية، فأعطاهما النصف كالواحدة، والجمهور على خلافه، وأن لفظ فَوْقَ زائدة كقوله: فَاضْرِبُوا فَوْقَ الْأَعْناقِ وقيل: أخذ الثلثين بالسُنة، وإن كانَتْ بنتًا واحِدَةً فَلَهَا النِّصْفُ، والباقي للعاصب، وفيه دليل على أنَّ الابن يأخذ جميع المال إذا انفرد لأن له مثل حظ الأنثيين.\nالإشارة: انظر البنت، إذا انفردت أخذت النصف، وإذا اجتمعت مع غيرها نقص لها، كذلك أمداد الأشياخ، من انفرد عندهم وحده، أخذ أكثر مما إذا اجتمع مع غيره، لانجماع نظر الشيخ إليه، وكان شيخنا رضي الله عنه يقول له شيخه: ما زال يأتيك الرجال- أي: إخوانك من الفقراء- وكان وحده، فيقول له: الله لا يجعل أحدًا يأتي حتى نشبع.\nوكذلك أيضًا، انفراد العبد بالعبادة، في وقت الغفلة، مددها أعظم من كونه مع غيره، كالمجاهد خلف الفارين.\nوكذلك قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «طوُبَى للغُرَبَاءِ» ، والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر ميراث الأبوين، فقال:\n... وَلِأَبَوَيْهِ لِكُلِّ واحِدٍ مِنْهُمَا السُّدُسُ مِمَّا تَرَكَ إِنْ كانَ لَهُ وَلَدٌ فَإِنْ لَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ وَلَدٌ وَوَرِثَهُ أَبَواهُ فَلِأُمِّهِ الثُّلُثُ فَإِنْ كانَ لَهُ إِخْوَةٌ فَلِأُمِّهِ السُّدُسُ مِنْ بَعْدِ وَصِيَّةٍ يُوصِي بِها أَوْ دَيْنٍ ...\nقلت: (السدس) مبتدأ، و (لأبويه) خبر، و (لكل واحد) ، بدل من (أبويه) ، ونكتة البدل إفادة أنهما لا يشتركان في السدس، ولو قال: لأبوَيْه السدس لأوهم الاشتراك.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إذا مات الولد، وترك أبويه، ف لِكُلِّ واحِدٍ مِنْهُمَا السُّدُسُ مِمَّا تَرَكَ إِنْ كانَ لَهُ وَلَدٌ ذكرًا أو أنثى، واحدًا أو متعددًا، للصلب أو ولد ابن، فكلهم يَردُّون الأبوين للسدس، فَإِنْ لَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ وَلَدٌ وَوَرِثَهُ أَبَواهُ فقط، فَلِأُمِّهِ الثُّلُثُ، والباقي للأب، فَإِنْ كانَ لَهُ إِخْوَةٌ أي: أخَوَان فأكثر، سواء كانوا أشقاء أو لأب أو لأم، أو مختلفين، فَلِأُمِّهِ السُّدُسُ، والباقي للأب، ولا شيء للأخوة معه.\nوأخذ ابن عباس بظاهر الآية، فلم يحجبها للسدس باثنين، وجعلهما كالواحد، واحتج بأن لفظ الإخوة جمع، وأقله ثلاثة، وأجيب بأن لفظ الجمع، يقع على الاثنين كقوله: وَكُنَّا لِحُكْمِهِمْ شاهِدِينَ، إِذْ تَسَوَّرُوا الْمِحْرابَ، ولقوله صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «الاثْنَانِ فَما فَوْقَهمَا جَمَاعَةٌ» . وهذا كله، بعد إخراج الوصية وقضاء الدين، وإنما قدّم الحق- تعالى- الوصية على الدَّين، مع كون الدّين مقدمًا في القضاء من رأس المال لأن أرباب الدّين أقوياء، بخلاف الموصى لهم، فقدمهم اعتناء بهم.\nالإشارة: الروح كالأب، والبشرية كالأم، وعقد الصحبة مع الشيخ كالولد، فإن كان الإنسان له صحبة مع شيخ التربية، يعني له ورد منه، فالبشرية والروحانية سواء، إذ كلاهما يتهذبان ويتنوران بالأدب والمعرفة الأدب للبشرية، والمعرفة للروحانية، إذا استمد بالطاعة الظاهرُ استمد الباطن، وبالعكس، وإن لم يكن عقد الصحبة موجودًا كان ميراث البشرية من الحس أقوى كميراث الأم مع فقد الولد، أو تقول: الإنسان مركب من حس ومعنى، فالحس كالأم، والمعنى كالأب، لأن المعاني قائمة بالحس، والروح تستمد منهما معًا، فهي كالولد بينهما، فإن كانت الروح حية بوجود المعرفة، استمدت منهما معًا، وإن كانت ميتة، كان استمدادها من الحس أكثر، كموت الولد في ميراث الأم.\nأو تقول: الإنسان بين قدرة وحكمة، القدرة كالأب، والحكمة كالأم، والقلب بينهما كالولد، فإِنْ وُجد القلب استمدت الروح من القدرة والحكمة، واستوى نظرها فيهما. وإن فقد القلب غلب على الروح ميراث الحكمة، كفَقْدِ\nالولد في ميراث الأم، وإن كان للقلب أخوة من الأنوار والأسرار يتقوى بهما فللروح من ميراث الحكمة السدس، والباقي كله للقدرة، ولا يعرف هذا إلا من حقق معرفة القدرة والحكمة، ذوقًا وكشفًا، وإلاَّ.. فليسلُم لأهل المعرفة.\nوالله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى حكمة تقسيم تركة الأب والابن على ما فرض، وأن ذلك لا يعلمه إلا هو، فقال:\n... آباؤُكُمْ وَأَبْناؤُكُمْ لاَ تَدْرُونَ أَيُّهُمْ أَقْرَبُ لَكُمْ نَفْعاً فَرِيضَةً مِنَ اللَّهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلِيماً حَكِيماً (11)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: قد بينت لكم ما يرثُ الأبُ من ابنه، وما يرث الولدُ من أبيه، ولو وكلت ذلك إليكم لأفسدتم القسمة لأنكم لا تدرون أيهم أقرب نفعًا للآخر، هل الأب أقرب نفعًا لابنه، فتعطوه الميراث كله دون ولد الميت، أو الولد أقرب نفعًا لأبيه، من الأب لابنه، فتخصونه بالإرث، ففرضتُ ميراث الأب وميراث الولد، ولم نكل ذلك إليكم. فَرِيضَةً حاصلة مِنَ اللَّهِ، إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلِيماً بمصالح العباد حَكِيماً بما فرض وقدَّر.\nوقال ابن عباس: لا تدرون أيهم أطوع لله عز وجل من الآباء والأبناء، وأرفعكم درجة يوم القيامة، لأن الله تعالى يشفع المؤمنين في بعضهم بعضًا، فيشفع الولد في والديه، إن كان أرفع درجة منهما، فيرفعهما الله إليه، ويشفع الوالدين في ولدهما، إن كانا أرفع درجة منه، فيرتفع إليهما لتقر بذلك أعينهما. هـ. بالمعنى.\nالإشارة: الإنسان لا تقوم روحانيته إلا ببشريته، وبشريته إلا بروحانيته، فلا يدري أيهما أقرب له نفعًا، لأن البشرية محل للعبودية، والروحانية محل لشهود عظمة الربوبية، ولا بد للجمع بينهما، وكذلك الحس، لا يقوم إلا بالمعنى، والمعنى لا يقوم إلا بالحس، فلا تدري أيهما أقرب نفعًا لك أيها المريد، فتؤثره، وإن كانت المعاني هي المقصودة بالسير، لكن لا تقوم إلا بوجود الحس، فلا بد من ملاحظته.\nوقال الورتجبي هنا ما نصه: أشكل الأمر من تلك الطائفتين، أيهم يبلغ درجة الولاية والمعرفة الموجبة مشاهدة الله وقربته، التي لو وقعت ذرة منها لأحد من هذه الأمة لينجو بشفاعته سبعون ألفًا بغير حساب، أي: اخدموا آباءكم وارحموا أولادكم، فربما يخرج منهم صاحب الولاية، ليشفع لكم عند الله تعالى، وحكمة الإبهام هاهنا ليشمل الرحمة والشفقة على الجمهور، لتوقع ذلك الولي الصادق. هـ. قلت: فسر الآباء والأبناء بالحسيين، وتشمل الآية أيضًا الآباء والأبناء المعنويين والروحانيين. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر ميراث الزوج والزوجة، فقال:","quran_ar":"يُوصِيكُمُ اللَّهُ فِي أَوْلادِكُمْ لِلذَّكَرِ مِثْلُ حَظِّ الْأُنْثَيَيْنِ فَإِنْ كُنَّ نِساءً فَوْقَ اثْنَتَيْنِ فَلَهُنَّ ثُلُثا مَا تَرَكَ وَإِنْ كانَتْ واحِدَةً فَلَهَا النِّصْفُ وَلِأَبَوَيْهِ لِكُلِّ واحِدٍ مِنْهُمَا السُّدُسُ مِمَّا تَرَكَ إِنْ كانَ لَهُ وَلَدٌ فَإِنْ لَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ وَلَدٌ وَوَرِثَهُ أَبَواهُ فَلِأُمِّهِ الثُّلُثُ فَإِنْ كانَ لَهُ إِخْوَةٌ فَلِأُمِّهِ السُّدُسُ مِنْ بَعْدِ وَصِيَّةٍ يُوصِي بِها أَوْ دَيْنٍ آباؤُكُمْ وَأَبْناؤُكُمْ لاَ تَدْرُونَ أَيُّهُمْ أَقْرَبُ لَكُمْ نَفْعاً فَرِيضَةً مِنَ اللَّهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلِيماً حَكِيماً (11)","quran_en":"11. God instructs you concerning your children: a male shall receive the equivalent of the share of two females. If the children are women, more than two, then they receive two-thirds of what is left; but if there is only one daughter, then hers is half. As for the deceased’s two parents, each of them receives a sixth of what is left if the deceased has a child. But if there is no child and the two parents inherit, his mother receives a third; if he has siblings, his mother receives a sixth—all after any bequest made or debt due. Your fathers and your children: you do not know which of them is nearer to you in benefit. This is an ordained allocation from God. Truly God is Knowing, Wise.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Shares of sons and daughters, and the report of their revelation","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—says: “God instructs you” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يُوصِيكُمُ اللَّهُ<\/bdi>), that is, commands you and entrusts you with a charge, “concerning your children” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فِي أَوْلَادِكُمْ<\/bdi>), meaning the specification of their inheritance. He then sets out the shares: “for the male the equivalent of the share of two females” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لِلذَّكَرِ مِثْلُ حَظِّ الْأُنْثَيَيْنِ<\/bdi>). In reckoning one male as two females, if a person leaves one son and one daughter, the estate is divided into three shares: two for the son and one for the daughter. If he leaves one son and two daughters, there are two portions for him and one for each of them. The same principle applies in analogous cases. Ibn Juzayy says that this verse was revealed on account of Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ; it is also said to concern Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh when the Messenger of God visited him in his illness. It abolished the pre-Islamic practice of withholding inheritance from women and children. Some say that it abrogated the earlier testamentary provision concerning parents and close relatives. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H012\">[H012]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—says: “God instructs you” ( يُوصِيكُمُ اللَّهُ ), that is, commands you and entrusts you with a charge, “concerning your children” ( فِي أَوْلَادِكُمْ ), meaning the specification of their inheritance. He then sets out the shares: “for the male the equivalent of the share of two females” ( لِلذَّكَرِ مِثْلُ حَظِّ الْأُنْثَيَيْنِ ). In reckoning one male as two females, if a person leaves one son and one daughter, the estate is divided into three shares: two for the son and one for the daughter. If he leaves one son and two daughters, there are two portions for him and one for each of them. The same principle applies in analogous cases. Ibn Juzayy says that this verse was revealed on account of Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ; it is also said to concern Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh when the Messenger of God visited him in his illness. It abolished the pre-Islamic practice of withholding inheritance from women and children. Some say that it abrogated the earlier testamentary provision concerning parents and close relatives. [H012]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Why “He instructs you,” why “children,” and why the male is named first","html":"<p>He said “God instructs you” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يُوصِيكُمُ اللَّهُ<\/bdi>) in the form of an ongoing verb rather than “God instructed you” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أَوْصَاكُمْ<\/bdi>), to call attention to the supersession of the previous ruling and the commencement of another. He used the explicit divine name “God” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اللَّهُ<\/bdi>) rather than “We instruct you” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نُوصِيكُمْ<\/bdi>) to magnify the importance of the injunction, bringing the greatest of the divine names. He said “concerning your children” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فِي أَوْلَادِكُمْ<\/bdi>) rather than “concerning your sons” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فِي أَبْنَائِكُمْ<\/bdi>), because the term “son” can also be used of a foster-son through suckling, the son of a daughter, or an adopted son, none of whom inherits on that basis alone. If someone asks why the formulation was not “the two females have the equivalent of the male’s share,” or “the female has half the male’s share,” the answer is that the male is put first because of the preference he is accorded [in this legal exposition], and because the purpose is to state his share; the reverse expression would entail preferring the females. Here ends the statement cited from Ibn Juzayy. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N100\">[N100]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He said “God instructs you” ( يُوصِيكُمُ اللَّهُ ) in the form of an ongoing verb rather than “God instructed you” ( أَوْصَاكُمْ ), to call attention to the supersession of the previous ruling and the commencement of another. He used the explicit divine name “God” ( اللَّهُ ) rather than “We instruct you” ( نُوصِيكُمْ ) to magnify the importance of the injunction, bringing the greatest of the divine names. He said “concerning your children” ( فِي أَوْلَادِكُمْ ) rather than “concerning your sons” ( فِي أَبْنَائِكُمْ ), because the term “son” can also be used of a foster-son through suckling, the son of a daughter, or an adopted son, none of whom inherits on that basis alone. If someone asks why the formulation was not “the two females have the equivalent of the male’s share,” or “the female has half the male’s share,” the answer is that the male is put first because of the preference he is accorded [in this legal exposition], and because the purpose is to state his share; the reverse expression would entail preferring the females. Here ends the statement cited from Ibn Juzayy. [N100]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The teacher’s attention and the distribution of spiritual support","html":"<p>Just as God has given instructions concerning the children of bodily humanity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أولاد البشرية<\/bdi>), so has He enjoined care concerning the children of spiritual parentage (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أولاد الروحانية<\/bdi>). Preference in distributing the flow of spiritual assistance (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإمداد<\/bdi>) depends upon the degree of esteem, love and affectionate care that the shaykh has for a disciple. In proportion to the place the disciple occupies in the shaykh’s heart, assistance flows to him. He may receive the share of two men or more, according to what was established for him in the eternal allotment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القسمة الأزلية<\/bdi>). And God, exalted is He, knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Just as God has given instructions concerning the children of bodily humanity ( أولاد البشرية ), so has He enjoined care concerning the children of spiritual parentage ( أولاد الروحانية ). Preference in distributing the flow of spiritual assistance ( الإمداد ) depends upon the degree of esteem, love and affectionate care that the shaykh has for a disciple. In proportion to the place the disciple occupies in the shaykh’s heart, assistance flows to him. He may receive the share of two men or more, according to what was established for him in the eternal allotment ( القسمة الأزلية ). And God, exalted is He, knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then mentioned the ruling concerning daughters where there are no sons, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then mentioned the ruling concerning daughters where there are no sons, saying:"},{"type":"language","title":"The feminine plural, and wāḥida in the nominative or accusative","html":"<p>I say: The pronoun in “if they are” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كُنَّ<\/bdi>) is feminine by reference to the predicate, or it refers to the [female children] left behind. The explanation given by al-Zamakhsharī is far-fetched. In the reading with “one” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وَاحِدَةُ<\/bdi>) in the nominative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرفع<\/bdi>), it is the subject (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فاعل<\/bdi>) of the complete <i>kāna<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كان التامة<\/bdi>); in the accusative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النصب<\/bdi>), it is the predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبر<\/bdi>) of <i>kāna<\/i>.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: The pronoun in “if they are” ( كُنَّ ) is feminine by reference to the predicate, or it refers to the [female children] left behind. The explanation given by al-Zamakhsharī is far-fetched. In the reading with “one” ( وَاحِدَةُ ) in the nominative ( الرفع ), it is the subject ( فاعل ) of the complete kāna ( كان التامة ); in the accusative ( النصب ), it is the predicate ( خبر ) of kāna ."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Two or more daughters and the dissent of Ibn ʿAbbās","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—says: If the children left behind are females, with no males among them, “above two” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فَوْقَ اثْنَتَيْنِ<\/bdi>), meaning two or more, “they have two-thirds of what he left,” and the residue belongs to the residuary heir (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العاصب<\/bdi>). Ibn ʿAbbās took the apparent sense of the verse literally, assigning two daughters one-half, as for a sole daughter. The majority disagreed with him, treating the expression “above” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فَوْقَ<\/bdi>) as additional, as in “strike above the necks” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فَاضْرِبُوا فَوْقَ الْأَعْنَاقِ<\/bdi>), or saying that the two-thirds share for two daughters is established by the Sunna. If the deceased leaves “one daughter” alone, “hers is half,” the remainder going to the residuary heir. This also indicates that a single son takes the entire estate when he is the only heir, since his share is the equivalent of that of two daughters.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—says: If the children left behind are females, with no males among them, “above two” ( فَوْقَ اثْنَتَيْنِ ), meaning two or more, “they have two-thirds of what he left,” and the residue belongs to the residuary heir ( العاصب ). Ibn ʿAbbās took the apparent sense of the verse literally, assigning two daughters one-half, as for a sole daughter. The majority disagreed with him, treating the expression “above” ( فَوْقَ ) as additional, as in “strike above the necks” ( فَاضْرِبُوا فَوْقَ الْأَعْنَاقِ ), or saying that the two-thirds share for two daughters is established by the Sunna. If the deceased leaves “one daughter” alone, “hers is half,” the remainder going to the residuary heir. This also indicates that a single son takes the entire estate when he is the only heir, since his share is the equivalent of that of two daughters."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Solitude in the presence of the teacher, and worship during heedlessness","html":"<p>Consider the daughter: on her own she receives half, while, when she is joined by others, her own portion is diminished. So, too, with the spiritual gifts (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أمداد<\/bdi>) of the shaykhs: a person who alone enjoys their attention may receive more than when others gather with him, because the shaykh’s gaze is concentrated upon him. Our shaykh—may God be pleased with him—said that his own shaykh used to tell him, when he was alone, “Men will go on coming to you,” meaning his fellow brethren among the poor (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفقراء<\/bdi>). He replied, “May God not let anyone come until we have eaten our fill.” Similarly, the assistance associated with a servant’s worship while others are heedless is greater than when he worships alongside them, just as the fighter who holds his ground behind those who flee. Likewise the Prophet said, “Glad tidings to the strangers” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">طُوبَى لِلْغُرَبَاءِ<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H010\">[H010]<\/a> And God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Consider the daughter: on her own she receives half, while, when she is joined by others, her own portion is diminished. So, too, with the spiritual gifts ( أمداد ) of the shaykhs: a person who alone enjoys their attention may receive more than when others gather with him, because the shaykh’s gaze is concentrated upon him. Our shaykh—may God be pleased with him—said that his own shaykh used to tell him, when he was alone, “Men will go on coming to you,” meaning his fellow brethren among the poor ( الفقراء ). He replied, “May God not let anyone come until we have eaten our fill.” Similarly, the assistance associated with a servant’s worship while others are heedless is greater than when he worships alongside them, just as the fighter who holds his ground behind those who flee. Likewise the Prophet said, “Glad tidings to the strangers” ( طُوبَى لِلْغُرَبَاءِ ). [H010] And God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then mentioned the inheritance of the parents, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then mentioned the inheritance of the parents, saying:"},{"type":"language","title":"A sixth for each: the function of badal","html":"<p>I say: “The sixth” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السُّدُسُ<\/bdi>) is a subject (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مبتدأ<\/bdi>), and “to his two parents” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لِأَبَوَيْهِ<\/bdi>) is a predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبر<\/bdi>). “To each one of them” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لِكُلِّ وَاحِدٍ مِنْهُمَا<\/bdi>) is a substitute or appositional replacement (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بدل<\/bdi>) for “to his two parents.” The point of this substitution is to show that they do not share one sixth between them; the wording “his two parents have the sixth” could have suggested that.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “The sixth” ( السُّدُسُ ) is a subject ( مبتدأ ), and “to his two parents” ( لِأَبَوَيْهِ ) is a predicate ( خبر ). “To each one of them” ( لِكُلِّ وَاحِدٍ مِنْهُمَا ) is a substitute or appositional replacement ( بدل ) for “to his two parents.” The point of this substitution is to show that they do not share one sixth between them; the wording “his two parents have the sixth” could have suggested that."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Both parents, siblings, wills and debts","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—says: If a child dies leaving both parents, “to each of them a sixth of what he left, if he has a child,” male or female, one or many, directly descended or the child of a son. All of these reduce each parent’s prescribed allotment to a sixth. But “if he has no child, and his two parents inherit from him” alone, “then his mother has a third,” and the rest goes to the father. “If he has siblings” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إِخْوَةٌ<\/bdi>), that is, two or more brothers or sisters—of the same father and mother, or of the father only, or mother only, or from several such categories—“then his mother has a sixth,” and the remainder goes to his father. The siblings themselves receive nothing while the father is present. Ibn ʿAbbās took the apparent expression “siblings” in its plural force, and did not reduce the mother to a sixth in the presence of two, regarding two as he would one: the minimum plural, on his view, is three. The response given is that a plural expression can be applied to two, as in the verses “And We witnessed their judgment” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وَكُنَّا لِحُكْمِهِمْ شَاهِدِينَ<\/bdi>) [Q 21:78] and “when they climbed over the wall of the sanctuary” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إِذْ تَسَوَّرُوا الْمِحْرَابَ<\/bdi>) [Q 38:21], and in the reported saying, “Two and more constitute a group” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاثنان فما فوقهما جماعة<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H011\">[H011]<\/a> All this takes effect after execution of the bequest and payment of the debt. God mentions the bequest before the debt, although a debt is satisfied first from the estate, because creditors already have strong legal claims, whereas those receiving a bequest are more vulnerable, so their interest is mentioned first out of special solicitude.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—says: If a child dies leaving both parents, “to each of them a sixth of what he left, if he has a child,” male or female, one or many, directly descended or the child of a son. All of these reduce each parent’s prescribed allotment to a sixth. But “if he has no child, and his two parents inherit from him” alone, “then his mother has a third,” and the rest goes to the father. “If he has siblings” ( إِخْوَةٌ ), that is, two or more brothers or sisters—of the same father and mother, or of the father only, or mother only, or from several such categories—“then his mother has a sixth,” and the remainder goes to his father. The siblings themselves receive nothing while the father is present. Ibn ʿAbbās took the apparent expression “siblings” in its plural force, and did not reduce the mother to a sixth in the presence of two, regarding two as he would one: the minimum plural, on his view, is three. The response given is that a plural expression can be applied to two, as in the verses “And We witnessed their judgment” ( وَكُنَّا لِحُكْمِهِمْ شَاهِدِينَ ) [Q 21:78] and “when they climbed over the wall of the sanctuary” ( إِذْ تَسَوَّرُوا الْمِحْرَابَ ) [Q 38:21], and in the reported saying, “Two and more constitute a group” ( الاثنان فما فوقهما جماعة ). [H011] All this takes effect after execution of the bequest and payment of the debt. God mentions the bequest before the debt, although a debt is satisfied first from the estate, because creditors already have strong legal claims, whereas those receiving a bequest are more vulnerable, so their interest is mentioned first out of special solicitude."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The father as spirit, the mother as embodied humanity, and companionship as a child","html":"<p>The spirit (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الروح<\/bdi>) is like the father, embodied humanity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البشرية<\/bdi>) like the mother, and the bond of companionship with the shaykh like a child. If someone has a bond of companionship with a shaykh of spiritual formation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شيخ التربية<\/bdi>)—meaning he has a regular practice received from him (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وِرْد<\/bdi>)—then his bodily and spiritual aspects receive equally: each is refined and illumined by proper conduct (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأدب<\/bdi>) and gnosis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المعرفة<\/bdi>). Conduct belongs to the bodily aspect and gnosis to the spiritual. When the outward draws assistance through obedience, the inward also receives it, and conversely. If there is no bond of companionship, bodily humanity receives a stronger inheritance from the sensory realm (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحس<\/bdi>), like the mother’s greater portion when there is no child. Or say: a human being is composed of the sensory (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حس<\/bdi>) and the intelligible or meaningful (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">معنى<\/bdi>). The sensory is like the mother, and meaning like the father, since meanings subsist in sensory things; the spirit draws aid from them both and thus is like their child. If the spirit is alive with gnosis, it draws from both together; but if dead, it receives more from the sensory, like the loss of a child [which increases] the mother’s inheritance. Or say: the human being stands between divine power (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القدرة<\/bdi>) and wisdom (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحكمة<\/bdi>). Power is like the father, wisdom like the mother, and the heart like the child between them. If the heart is present, the spirit draws from both power and wisdom, and its regard toward both becomes equal. If the heart is absent, wisdom’s inheritance predominates in the spirit, just as in the mother’s inheritance when the child is absent. If the heart has brethren among lights and secrets that strengthen it, then the spirit takes a sixth from the inheritance of wisdom and the rest belongs entirely to power. This is intelligible only to one who has realized knowledge of power and wisdom by direct taste (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ذوق<\/bdi>) and unveiling (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كشف<\/bdi>). Otherwise, one should defer to the people of gnosis. And God, exalted is He, knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The spirit ( الروح ) is like the father, embodied humanity ( البشرية ) like the mother, and the bond of companionship with the shaykh like a child. If someone has a bond of companionship with a shaykh of spiritual formation ( شيخ التربية )—meaning he has a regular practice received from him ( وِرْد )—then his bodily and spiritual aspects receive equally: each is refined and illumined by proper conduct ( الأدب ) and gnosis ( المعرفة ). Conduct belongs to the bodily aspect and gnosis to the spiritual. When the outward draws assistance through obedience, the inward also receives it, and conversely. If there is no bond of companionship, bodily humanity receives a stronger inheritance from the sensory realm ( الحس ), like the mother’s greater portion when there is no child. Or say: a human being is composed of the sensory ( حس ) and the intelligible or meaningful ( معنى ). The sensory is like the mother, and meaning like the father, since meanings subsist in sensory things; the spirit draws aid from them both and thus is like their child. If the spirit is alive with gnosis, it draws from both together; but if dead, it receives more from the sensory, like the loss of a child [which increases] the mother’s inheritance. Or say: the human being stands between divine power ( القدرة ) and wisdom ( الحكمة ). Power is like the father, wisdom like the mother, and the heart like the child between them. If the heart is present, the spirit draws from both power and wisdom, and its regard toward both becomes equal. If the heart is absent, wisdom’s inheritance predominates in the spirit, just as in the mother’s inheritance when the child is absent. If the heart has brethren among lights and secrets that strengthen it, then the spirit takes a sixth from the inheritance of wisdom and the rest belongs entirely to power. This is intelligible only to one who has realized knowledge of power and wisdom by direct taste ( ذوق ) and unveiling ( كشف ). Otherwise, one should defer to the people of gnosis. And God, exalted is He, knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>God then disclosed the wisdom in His allotment of the father’s and son’s inheritance, which He alone knows: “Your fathers and your sons…”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"God then disclosed the wisdom in His allotment of the father’s and son’s inheritance, which He alone knows: “Your fathers and your sons…”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Why God alone assigns the shares","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—says: I have explained what a father inherits from his son and what a son inherits from his father. Had I left the matter to you, you would have spoiled the division: you do not know which is closer to the other in benefit. Is the father more beneficial to his son, so you should give him the whole inheritance to the exclusion of the dead man’s child? Or is a child of greater benefit to his father than a father is to his child, so that you should reserve the inheritance for him? I therefore ordained the shares of father and son and did not leave the decision to you. An allotted portion “ordained by God” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فَرِيضَةً مِنَ اللَّهِ<\/bdi>): truly God knows His servants’ interests and is wise in what He apportions and determines. Ibn ʿAbbās said: You do not know who among your parents and children is more obedient to God or more exalted in rank on the Day of Resurrection, for God permits believers to intercede for one another. A child whose station is higher than that of his parents intercedes for them and God raises them to his rank. Parents more exalted than their child likewise intercede for him, so that he is raised to them and their eyes are gladdened. Here ends the substance, paraphrased, of Ibn ʿAbbās’s statement.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—says: I have explained what a father inherits from his son and what a son inherits from his father. Had I left the matter to you, you would have spoiled the division: you do not know which is closer to the other in benefit. Is the father more beneficial to his son, so you should give him the whole inheritance to the exclusion of the dead man’s child? Or is a child of greater benefit to his father than a father is to his child, so that you should reserve the inheritance for him? I therefore ordained the shares of father and son and did not leave the decision to you. An allotted portion “ordained by God” ( فَرِيضَةً مِنَ اللَّهِ ): truly God knows His servants’ interests and is wise in what He apportions and determines. Ibn ʿAbbās said: You do not know who among your parents and children is more obedient to God or more exalted in rank on the Day of Resurrection, for God permits believers to intercede for one another. A child whose station is higher than that of his parents intercedes for them and God raises them to his rank. Parents more exalted than their child likewise intercede for him, so that he is raised to them and their eyes are gladdened. Here ends the substance, paraphrased, of Ibn ʿAbbās’s statement."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Embodiment and spirit: neither can dispense with the other","html":"<p>A person’s spiritual reality (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">روحانية<\/bdi>) cannot stand without his bodily humanity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بشرية<\/bdi>), nor bodily humanity without spirituality. So one does not know which of the two is nearer to him in benefit: bodily humanity is the locus for servanthood (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العبودية<\/bdi>), whereas spirituality is the locus for witnessing the majesty of Lordship (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الربوبية<\/bdi>). Both must be joined. Likewise sensory manifestation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحس<\/bdi>) cannot subsist without meaning (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المعنى<\/bdi>), nor meaning without sensory manifestation; you, seeker (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مريد<\/bdi>), do not know which is more beneficial to you so as to prefer it. Although meanings are what one journeys towards, they cannot be established without the sensory; it must therefore be attended to. Al-Wartajbī writes here, in his own words: “The question concerning these two parties has been left obscure: which of them will attain the station of sainthood (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الولاية<\/bdi>) and gnosis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المعرفة<\/bdi>) that yields witnessing of God and nearness to Him? Were a single atom of it to fall to anyone in this community, seventy thousand might be delivered by his intercession without reckoning. In other words: serve your fathers and be tender towards your children, for from among them may emerge a person endowed with sainthood, who will intercede for you before God. The wisdom of leaving the matter unspecified here is to extend kindness and compassion throughout the community in anticipation of the true friend of God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الولي الصادق<\/bdi>).” Here ends his statement. I say: He interprets fathers and sons as those related by bodily kinship, but the verse also embraces fathers and sons in meaning and in spiritual relationship. And God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"A person’s spiritual reality ( روحانية ) cannot stand without his bodily humanity ( بشرية ), nor bodily humanity without spirituality. So one does not know which of the two is nearer to him in benefit: bodily humanity is the locus for servanthood ( العبودية ), whereas spirituality is the locus for witnessing the majesty of Lordship ( الربوبية ). Both must be joined. Likewise sensory manifestation ( الحس ) cannot subsist without meaning ( المعنى ), nor meaning without sensory manifestation; you, seeker ( مريد ), do not know which is more beneficial to you so as to prefer it. Although meanings are what one journeys towards, they cannot be established without the sensory; it must therefore be attended to. Al-Wartajbī writes here, in his own words: “The question concerning these two parties has been left obscure: which of them will attain the station of sainthood ( الولاية ) and gnosis ( المعرفة ) that yields witnessing of God and nearness to Him? Were a single atom of it to fall to anyone in this community, seventy thousand might be delivered by his intercession without reckoning. In other words: serve your fathers and be tender towards your children, for from among them may emerge a person endowed with sainthood, who will intercede for you before God. The wisdom of leaving the matter unspecified here is to extend kindness and compassion throughout the community in anticipation of the true friend of God ( الولي الصادق ).” Here ends his statement. I say: He interprets fathers and sons as those related by bodily kinship, but the verse also embraces fathers and sons in meaning and in spiritual relationship. And God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then mentioned the inheritance of husbands and wives.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then mentioned the inheritance of husbands and wives."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C472.1","page":472,"ar":"الكلام السابق من كلام ابن جزي.","en":"The preceding discussion is from Ibn Juzayy.","anchor":"لكان فيه تفضيل للإناث. هـ"}],"english_characters":12281,"arabic_characters":6895},{"id":"q4-12","q":"4:12","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:12 — Spousal inheritance, the meaning of kalāla, and the shared inheritance of spiritual brethren","source":"Cairo Arabic edition I:475–477","cairo":"I:475–477","source_page_first":475,"source_page_last":477,"ar":"وَلَكُمْ نِصْفُ مَا تَرَكَ أَزْواجُكُمْ إِنْ لَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُنَّ وَلَدٌ فَإِنْ كانَ لَهُنَّ وَلَدٌ فَلَكُمُ الرُّبُعُ مِمَّا تَرَكْنَ مِنْ بَعْدِ وَصِيَّةٍ يُوصِينَ بِها أَوْ دَيْنٍ وَلَهُنَّ الرُّبُعُ مِمَّا تَرَكْتُمْ إِنْ لَمْ يَكُنْ لَكُمْ وَلَدٌ فَإِنْ كانَ لَكُمْ وَلَدٌ فَلَهُنَّ الثُّمُنُ مِمَّا تَرَكْتُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ وَصِيَّةٍ تُوصُونَ بِها أَوْ دَيْنٍ ...\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلَكُمْ أيها الأزواج، من ميراث أزواجكم نِصْفُ ما تركن إِنْ لَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُنَّ وَلَدٌ. فَإِنْ كانَ لَهُنَّ وَلَدٌ وارث، ذكرًا أو أنثى، مفردًا أو متعددًا، من بطنها أو من صلب بنيها أو بني بنيها وإن سفل، منكم أو من غيركم، فَلَكُمُ الرُّبُعُ مِمَّا تَرَكْنَ، بعد قضاء الدين وإخراج الوصية.\nوَلَهُنَّ أي: الزوجات من ميراث الزوج الرُّبُعُ مما ترك إِنْ لَمْ يَكُنْ له ولد لاحق، ذكرًا أو أنثى، على وزان ما تقدم في الزوجة، فَإِنْ كانَ لَكُمْ وَلَدٌ فَلَهُنَّ الثُّمُنُ تنفرد به إن كانت واحدة، ويُقسم بينهن إن تعددن، ولا ينقص لأهل السهام مما فرض الله لهم إلا ما نقصه العَوْل على مذهب الجمهور، خلافًا لابن عباس، فإنه لا يقول بالعول.\nفإن قيل: لِمَ كرر قوله: مِنْ بَعْدِ وَصِيَّةٍ مع ميراث الزوج وميراث الزوجة، ولم يذكره قبل ذلك إلا مرة واحدة في ميراث الأولاد والأبوين؟ فالجواب: أن الموروث في ميراث الزوج هو الزوجة، والموروث في ميراث الزوجة هو الزوج، فكل واحدة قضية مستقلة، فلذلك ذكر ذلك مع كل واحدة، بخلاف الأول فأن الموروث فيه واحد، ذكرَ حكم ما يرث منه أولاده وأبواه، وهي قضية واحدة، فلذلك قال فيه: مِنْ بَعْدِ وَصِيَّةٍ مرة واحدة.\nقاله ابن جزي. قال البيضاوي: فرض للرجل بحق الزواج ضِعف ما للمرأة كما في النسب، وهكذا قياس كل رجل وامرأة، إذا اشتركا في الجهة والقرب، ولا يستثنى منه إلا أولاد الأم والمعتق والمعتقة. هـ.\nالإشارة: إذا ماتت النفس، ولم تبق لها بقية، وورثت الروحُ ما كان لها من العلوم الكسبية: النقلية والعقلية، وأضافته إلى مَالهَا من العلوم الوهبية، فانقلب الجميع وهبيًا، قال بعض شيوخ أشياخنا: (كنتُ أعرف أربعة عشر علمًا، فلما دخلت علم الحقيقة سرطت ذلك كله، فلم يبق إلا الكتاب والسنة) ، أو كما قال. وقال أبو سليمان الداراني:\nإذا اعتقدت النفوس على ترك الآثام، جالت في الملكوت، ثم عادت إلى صاحبها بطرائف العلوم، من غير أن يؤدي إليها عالم علما.\nفإن بقي للنفس بقية، نقص ميراث الروح منها، بقدر البقية، كما أن الزوج ينقص ميراثه مع الفرع، وكذلك إذا ماتت الروح بالرجوع عن طريق الجِد، ورثت النفسُ ما كان لها من العلوم الوهبية، والمعاني والأسرار القدسية، فتأكلها، وتردها نقلية حسية، بعد أن كانت وهبية ذوقية، فتتحسس المعاني، وتتكثف الأواني. والعياذ بالله من السلب بعد العطاء، إلا أن ميراث النفس من الروح أقوى، فإن بقي للروح شيء من الحياة، نقص ميراث النفس منها، كنقص الزوجة مع الفرع من ميراث الزوج، والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر ميراث الأخ للأخ، فقال:\n... وَإِنْ كانَ رَجُلٌ يُورَثُ كَلالَةً أَوِ امْرَأَةٌ وَلَهُ أَخٌ أَوْ أُخْتٌ فَلِكُلِّ واحِدٍ مِنْهُمَا السُّدُسُ فَإِنْ كانُوا أَكْثَرَ مِنْ ذلِكَ فَهُمْ شُرَكاءُ فِي الثُّلُثِ مِنْ بَعْدِ وَصِيَّةٍ يُوصى بِها أَوْ دَيْنٍ غَيْرَ مُضَارٍّ وَصِيَّةً مِنَ اللَّهِ وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ حَلِيمٌ (12)\nقلت: الكلالة: انقطاع النسل، بحيث لم يبق للميت فرع ولا أصل، لا ذكر ولا أنثى، وهو مصدر من تَكَلَّلهُ النسبُ، إذا أحاط به كالإكليل، لأن ورثته أحاطوا به ولَيْسوا منه. ونظم بعضهم معنى الكلالة، فقال:\nإن امرؤٌ يَسْأَلُ عن كَلاَلَة ... هو انْقِطاعُ النَّسْلِ لا مَحَالة\nلا والدُ يَبْقَى ولا مولوُد ... قدْ هَلَكَ الأبْنَاءُ والجُدُود\nفتحتمل أن تطلق هنا على الميت، أو على الورثة، أو على الوراثة، أو على القرابة أو على المال. فإن كانت على الميت، فإعرابه خبر كان، و (يورث) صفة، أو (يورث) خبر كان، و (كلالة) حال من الضمير في (يورث) ، أو «كان» تامة، و (يورث) صفة و (كلالة) حال من الضمير. وإن كانت على الورثة، فهو خبر كان، على حذف مضاف أي: ذا كلالة، وإن كانت الوِرَاثة فهو مصدر في موضع الحال، وإن كانت القرابة، فهو مفعول من أجله، أي: يورث من أجل القرابة. وإن كانت للمال، فهو مفعول ثانِ ليورث، وكل من هذه يحتمل أن تكون «كان» تامة أو ناقصة. قاله ابن جزي. و (غير مضار) ، منصوب على الحال، أو العامل فيه (يوصى) ، و (مضار) اسم فاعل، ووصية: مصدر ليوصي، أو مفعول (مضار) .\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وإن كان الميت رجلاً أو امرأة، يُورثان كلالة، بحيث لا فرع لهما ولا أصل، قد انقطع عمود نسبهما، ولهما أخ أو أخت لأم فَلِكُلِّ واحِدٍ مِنْهُمَا السُّدُسُ. فَإِنْ كانُوا أَكْثَرَ مِنْ ذلِكَ فَهُمْ شُرَكاءُ فِي الثُّلُثِ\n، الذكر والأنثى سواء، لأن الإدلاء للميت بمحض الأنوثة، ومفهوم الآية: أنهما لا يرثان مع الأم والجدة، كما لا يرثان مع البنت وبنت الابن، إذ ليس حينئذٍ بكلالة، وإنما قيدنا الأخ والأخت بكونهما للأم لأن الأخ الشقيق أو للأب سيأتي في آخر السورة. والأخت تقدم أنَّ لها النصف، وأيضًا: قد قرأ سعد بن أبي وقاص، وابن مسعود: «وله أخ أو أخت لأم» .\nوهذا كله مِنْ بَعْدِ وَصِيَّةٍ يُوصى بِها أَوْ دَيْنٍ حال كونه غَيْرَ مُضَارٍّ في الوصية أو الدين، كالوصية بأكثر من الثلث، أو للوارث، أو فِرارًا منه، فإن عُلِم أنه قصد الإضرار، رد ما زاد على الثلث، واختلف في رد الثلث على قولين. قاله ابن جزي. وَصِيَّةً مِنَ اللَّهِ، أي: نوصيكم وصية، أو غير مضار وصية من الله. قال ابن عباس: (الإضرار في الوصية من الكبائر) . وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ بمصالح عباده، يقسم المال على حسب المصلحة، حَلِيمٌ لا يُعاجِل بالعقوبة من خالف حدوده.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن الأخوة في الشيخ كالأخوة في النسب، لأنهم يرضعون من ثدي واحدة ولبن واحد، فإن مات أحدهم، ورث أخوه المدد الذي كان يأخذه من شيخه، وكذا إذا رجع- فإنه موت- فينقلب المدد إلى أخيه، ومثاله كماء فُرِّقَ على قواديس، فإذا انسدت إحدى القواديس رجع الماء إلى الأخرى، فإن كانوا أكثر من واحد فهم شركاء في ذلك المدد، والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم حذَّر الحق- تعالى- من مخالفة ما حدّ فى الوصايا والمواريث، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلَكُمْ نِصْفُ مَا تَرَكَ أَزْواجُكُمْ إِنْ لَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُنَّ وَلَدٌ فَإِنْ كانَ لَهُنَّ وَلَدٌ فَلَكُمُ الرُّبُعُ مِمَّا تَرَكْنَ مِنْ بَعْدِ وَصِيَّةٍ يُوصِينَ بِها أَوْ دَيْنٍ وَلَهُنَّ الرُّبُعُ مِمَّا تَرَكْتُمْ إِنْ لَمْ يَكُنْ لَكُمْ وَلَدٌ فَإِنْ كانَ لَكُمْ وَلَدٌ فَلَهُنَّ الثُّمُنُ مِمَّا تَرَكْتُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ وَصِيَّةٍ تُوصُونَ بِها أَوْ دَيْنٍ وَإِنْ كانَ رَجُلٌ يُورَثُ كَلالَةً أَوِ امْرَأَةٌ وَلَهُ أَخٌ أَوْ أُخْتٌ فَلِكُلِّ واحِدٍ مِنْهُمَا السُّدُسُ فَإِنْ كانُوا أَكْثَرَ مِنْ ذلِكَ فَهُمْ شُرَكاءُ فِي الثُّلُثِ مِنْ بَعْدِ وَصِيَّةٍ يُوصى بِها أَوْ دَيْنٍ غَيْرَ مُضَارٍّ وَصِيَّةً مِنَ اللَّهِ وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ حَلِيمٌ (12)","quran_en":"12. You, husbands, receive half of what your wives leave if they have no child. But if they have a child, yours is a quarter of what they leave, after any bequest they have made or debt. And wives receive a quarter of what you leave if you have no child; but if you have a child, theirs is an eighth of what you leave, after any bequest you have made or debt. If a man or woman is inherited from as one leaving neither direct descendants nor ascendants (kalāla), and has a brother or sister, each of them receives a sixth; but if there are more than that, they share in a third, after any bequest made or debt, without causing injury. This is an injunction from God; and God is Knowing, Forbearing.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The husband’s and wife’s prescribed shares","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—says: “To you,” husbands, from the inheritance of your wives, belongs “half of what they leave, if they have no child.” But if they have a child entitled to inherit, male or female, one or several—whether born directly of her or descended through sons, sons’ sons and so on, whether related to you or to another husband—“yours is a quarter of what they leave,” after the debt is discharged and the bequest executed. “And to them,” the wives, belongs “a quarter” of what the husband leaves if he has no descendant entitled to inherit, male or female, on the same basis as stated previously regarding the wife. But “if you have a child, theirs is an eighth,” which a single wife takes alone, or which several wives divide among themselves. The prescribed shares (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السهام<\/bdi>) are not diminished except by proportional adjustment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العَوْل<\/bdi>), according to the majority school, contrary to Ibn ʿAbbās, who did not accept <i>ʿawl<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العَوْل<\/bdi>). If it is asked why the clause “after any bequest” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مِنْ بَعْدِ وَصِيَّةٍ<\/bdi>) is repeated for the inheritance of both husband and wife, while it was mentioned only once with the children’s and parents’ inheritance in the preceding verse, the answer is that, in the case of the husband’s inheritance, the deceased is his wife, while, in the wife’s inheritance, the deceased is her husband. Each therefore constitutes a separate legal case, and the clause appears with each. In the former case, the deceased was one person whose children’s and parents’ shares were set out together, so it was mentioned once. This is Ibn Juzayy’s explanation. Al-Bayḍāwī says: The man is assigned twice the share of a woman by virtue of marriage, as in kinship. Such is the analogy wherever a man and woman share the same cause and proximity of inheritance, except for siblings through the mother and for emancipators, male and female. Here ends his statement.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—says: “To you,” husbands, from the inheritance of your wives, belongs “half of what they leave, if they have no child.” But if they have a child entitled to inherit, male or female, one or several—whether born directly of her or descended through sons, sons’ sons and so on, whether related to you or to another husband—“yours is a quarter of what they leave,” after the debt is discharged and the bequest executed. “And to them,” the wives, belongs “a quarter” of what the husband leaves if he has no descendant entitled to inherit, male or female, on the same basis as stated previously regarding the wife. But “if you have a child, theirs is an eighth,” which a single wife takes alone, or which several wives divide among themselves. The prescribed shares ( السهام ) are not diminished except by proportional adjustment ( العَوْل ), according to the majority school, contrary to Ibn ʿAbbās, who did not accept ʿawl ( العَوْل ). If it is asked why the clause “after any bequest” ( مِنْ بَعْدِ وَصِيَّةٍ ) is repeated for the inheritance of both husband and wife, while it was mentioned only once with the children’s and parents’ inheritance in the preceding verse, the answer is that, in the case of the husband’s inheritance, the deceased is his wife, while, in the wife’s inheritance, the deceased is her husband. Each therefore constitutes a separate legal case, and the clause appears with each. In the former case, the deceased was one person whose children’s and parents’ shares were set out together, so it was mentioned once. This is Ibn Juzayy’s explanation. Al-Bayḍāwī says: The man is assigned twice the share of a woman by virtue of marriage, as in kinship. Such is the analogy wherever a man and woman share the same cause and proximity of inheritance, except for siblings through the mother and for emancipators, male and female. Here ends his statement."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"When the bodily self dies and acquired knowledge becomes freely bestowed","html":"<p>When the self (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النفس<\/bdi>) dies and no remainder of it survives, the spirit (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الروح<\/bdi>) inherits what it possessed of acquired knowledge (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العلوم الكسبية<\/bdi>), whether transmitted (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نقلية<\/bdi>) or rational (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عقلية<\/bdi>). It joins this to its own freely bestowed knowledge (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العلوم الوهبية<\/bdi>), and all is transformed into something bestowed. One of our earlier masters’ masters said: “I used to know fourteen sciences. When I entered the science of Reality (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">علم الحقيقة<\/bdi>), I swallowed all of that, until only the Book and Sunna remained”—or words to that effect. Abū Sulaymān al-Dārānī said: “When souls resolve to give up sins, they travel through the Dominion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الملكوت<\/bdi>) and return to their owners bearing wondrous sciences, though no learned person transmitted knowledge to them.” If some remainder of the self subsists, the spirit’s inheritance of its knowledge decreases in proportion to that remainder, just as the husband’s share decreases when his wife leaves a descendant. Conversely, if the spirit dies by turning back from the path of earnest striving (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">طريق الجدّ<\/bdi>), the self inherits the freely bestowed sciences, meanings and sacred mysteries that had belonged to it. It consumes them and transforms them into things transmitted and sensory after they had been freely bestowed and tasted directly. It makes meanings sensory and vessels dense. We seek God’s protection from dispossession after having received a gift (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السلب بعد العطاء<\/bdi>). Yet the self’s inheritance from the spirit is stronger: if something remains of the spirit’s life, the self’s share is reduced, as the wife’s inheritance from her husband decreases when he leaves a descendant. And God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"When the self ( النفس ) dies and no remainder of it survives, the spirit ( الروح ) inherits what it possessed of acquired knowledge ( العلوم الكسبية ), whether transmitted ( نقلية ) or rational ( عقلية ). It joins this to its own freely bestowed knowledge ( العلوم الوهبية ), and all is transformed into something bestowed. One of our earlier masters’ masters said: “I used to know fourteen sciences. When I entered the science of Reality ( علم الحقيقة ), I swallowed all of that, until only the Book and Sunna remained”—or words to that effect. Abū Sulaymān al-Dārānī said: “When souls resolve to give up sins, they travel through the Dominion ( الملكوت ) and return to their owners bearing wondrous sciences, though no learned person transmitted knowledge to them.” If some remainder of the self subsists, the spirit’s inheritance of its knowledge decreases in proportion to that remainder, just as the husband’s share decreases when his wife leaves a descendant. Conversely, if the spirit dies by turning back from the path of earnest striving ( طريق الجدّ ), the self inherits the freely bestowed sciences, meanings and sacred mysteries that had belonged to it. It consumes them and transforms them into things transmitted and sensory after they had been freely bestowed and tasted directly. It makes meanings sensory and vessels dense. We seek God’s protection from dispossession after having received a gift ( السلب بعد العطاء ). Yet the self’s inheritance from the spirit is stronger: if something remains of the spirit’s life, the self’s share is reduced, as the wife’s inheritance from her husband decreases when he leaves a descendant. And God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then mentions the inheritance of a sibling from a sibling: “And if a man …”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then mentions the inheritance of a sibling from a sibling: “And if a man …”"},{"type":"language","title":"Kalāla (الكلالة), the network of kinship and alternative syntactic analyses","html":"<p>I say: <i>Kalāla<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الكَلَالَة<\/bdi>) is the severance of the line of descent, such that the deceased has neither descendants nor ascendants, male or female. It is a verbal noun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدر<\/bdi>) derived from the expression “kinship encircled him” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تكلّله النسبُ<\/bdi>), like a wreath (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإكليل<\/bdi>), because his heirs surround him rather than issuing from him. A poet gave its meaning in verse:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">If someone asks about kalāla,<br/>It is the severance of the line of descent, without doubt:<br/>No parent is left and no offspring,<br/>Sons and grandfathers have passed away.<\/blockquote><p>The expression may here denote the deceased, the heirs, the act of inheritance, kinship itself, or the property inherited. If it denotes the deceased, it is the predicate (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خبر<\/bdi>) of <i>kāna<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كان<\/bdi>) while “is inherited from” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يُورَثُ<\/bdi>) is an adjective (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صفة<\/bdi>). Alternatively, “is inherited from” is the predicate of <i>kāna<\/i> and <i>kalāla<\/i> is a circumstantial qualifier (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال<\/bdi>) of its implicit pronoun, or <i>kāna<\/i> is complete (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تامة<\/bdi>), with “is inherited from” adjectival and <i>kalāla<\/i> again a circumstantial qualifier. If it denotes the heirs, it is the predicate of <i>kāna<\/i> with a suppressed annexed noun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مضاف محذوف<\/bdi>): “one possessed of [the relationship of] kalāla” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ذا كلالة<\/bdi>). If it denotes the inheritance, it is a verbal noun functioning as a circumstantial qualifier. If it denotes kinship, it is an accusative of cause (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول لأجله<\/bdi>)—one is inherited from by reason of the kinship. If it denotes the property, it is the second object (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول ثانٍ<\/bdi>) of “is inherited from.” Each construction admits <i>kāna<\/i> as complete or incomplete. This is Ibn Juzayy’s grammatical analysis. The phrase “without causing injury” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غَيْرَ مُضَارٍّ<\/bdi>) is accusative as a circumstantial qualifier (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال<\/bdi>), or its governor (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عامل<\/bdi>) is “he enjoins [a bequest]” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يُوصَى<\/bdi>). “One causing injury” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مُضَارّ<\/bdi>) is an active participle (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اسم فاعل<\/bdi>). “A bequest” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وَصِيَّةً<\/bdi>) is a verbal noun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدر<\/bdi>) of “enjoins,” or the object (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول<\/bdi>) of “causing injury.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Kalāla ( الكَلَالَة ) is the severance of the line of descent, such that the deceased has neither descendants nor ascendants, male or female. It is a verbal noun ( مصدر ) derived from the expression “kinship encircled him” ( تكلّله النسبُ ), like a wreath ( الإكليل ), because his heirs surround him rather than issuing from him. A poet gave its meaning in verse: If someone asks about kalāla, It is the severance of the line of descent, without doubt: No parent is left and no offspring, Sons and grandfathers have passed away. The expression may here denote the deceased, the heirs, the act of inheritance, kinship itself, or the property inherited. If it denotes the deceased, it is the predicate ( خبر ) of kāna ( كان ) while “is inherited from” ( يُورَثُ ) is an adjective ( صفة ). Alternatively, “is inherited from” is the predicate of kāna and kalāla is a circumstantial qualifier ( حال ) of its implicit pronoun, or kāna is complete ( تامة ), with “is inherited from” adjectival and kalāla again a circumstantial qualifier. If it denotes the heirs, it is the predicate of kāna with a suppressed annexed noun ( مضاف محذوف ): “one possessed of [the relationship of] kalāla” ( ذا كلالة ). If it denotes the inheritance, it is a verbal noun functioning as a circumstantial qualifier. If it denotes kinship, it is an accusative of cause ( مفعول لأجله )—one is inherited from by reason of the kinship. If it denotes the property, it is the second object ( مفعول ثانٍ ) of “is inherited from.” Each construction admits kāna as complete or incomplete. This is Ibn Juzayy’s grammatical analysis. The phrase “without causing injury” ( غَيْرَ مُضَارٍّ ) is accusative as a circumstantial qualifier ( حال ), or its governor ( عامل ) is “he enjoins [a bequest]” ( يُوصَى ). “One causing injury” ( مُضَارّ ) is an active participle ( اسم فاعل ). “A bequest” ( وَصِيَّةً ) is a verbal noun ( مصدر ) of “enjoins,” or the object ( مفعول ) of “causing injury.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Maternal siblings, equal shares and harmful wills","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—says: If the deceased, a man or woman, is inherited from as a <i>kalāla<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كَلَالَة<\/bdi>), with neither descendant nor ascendant—his direct line of descent having ended—and has a brother or sister <strong>through the mother<\/strong>, “each of them has a sixth.” But “if they are more numerous, they are partners in a third”; male and female take equal shares, because their link with the deceased runs through the maternal line alone. The implication is that they do not inherit alongside the mother or grandmother, nor alongside a daughter or son’s daughter, since the deceased is then not a <i>kalāla<\/i> in the required sense. [Source and textual note: This is a prima facie inference (<em>mafhūm<\/em>), not the final inheritance rule. Al-Bayḍāwī on 4:12 gives the same “mother and grandmother” wording, then adds that the implication is restricted by consensus. That qualification is absent from Cairo I:477. Mālik’s <em>Muwaṭṭaʾ<\/em>, Book of Inheritances, chapter on maternal siblings, excludes those siblings in the presence of descendants, the father or paternal grandfather, and allows them to inherit otherwise. The earlier parallel therefore explains the wording without replacing “mother and grandmother” by “father and grandfather.” The origin of Cairo’s omitted qualification remains unestablished.] We restrict brother and sister here to those related through the mother because the full or paternal brother’s inheritance will be explained at the end of the sūrah. The sister’s half-share has already been mentioned. Furthermore, Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and Ibn Masʿūd read: “and he has a brother or sister <strong>through the mother<\/strong>” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وله أخ أو أخت لأم<\/bdi>). All these shares are distributed “after any bequest made or debt,” on condition that no harm is caused (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غَيْرَ مُضَارٍّ<\/bdi>) through bequest or debt—for example, a bequest of more than a third, an unauthorized testamentary allocation to an heir, or a contrivance designed to defeat others’ rights. Where the intention to inflict harm is known, any excess above a third is set aside; the jurists disagreed, in two reported opinions, whether the third itself should also be invalidated. This is Ibn Juzayy’s account. “A bequest from God” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وَصِيَّةً مِنَ اللَّهِ<\/bdi>) means “We enjoin this upon you as an injunction,” or means “without violating an injunction from God.” Ibn ʿAbbās said, “Causing harm in a bequest is one of the grave sins.” God knows what serves His servants’ welfare, apportions wealth accordingly, and is Forbearing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حَلِيمٌ<\/bdi>): He does not immediately inflict punishment upon those who violate His bounds.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—says: If the deceased, a man or woman, is inherited from as a kalāla ( كَلَالَة ), with neither descendant nor ascendant—his direct line of descent having ended—and has a brother or sister through the mother , “each of them has a sixth.” But “if they are more numerous, they are partners in a third”; male and female take equal shares, because their link with the deceased runs through the maternal line alone. The implication is that they do not inherit alongside the mother or grandmother, nor alongside a daughter or son’s daughter, since the deceased is then not a kalāla in the required sense. [Source and textual note: This is a prima facie inference ( mafhūm ), not the final inheritance rule. Al-Bayḍāwī on 4:12 gives the same “mother and grandmother” wording, then adds that the implication is restricted by consensus. That qualification is absent from Cairo I:477. Mālik’s Muwaṭṭaʾ , Book of Inheritances, chapter on maternal siblings, excludes those siblings in the presence of descendants, the father or paternal grandfather, and allows them to inherit otherwise. The earlier parallel therefore explains the wording without replacing “mother and grandmother” by “father and grandfather.” The origin of Cairo’s omitted qualification remains unestablished.] We restrict brother and sister here to those related through the mother because the full or paternal brother’s inheritance will be explained at the end of the sūrah. The sister’s half-share has already been mentioned. Furthermore, Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and Ibn Masʿūd read: “and he has a brother or sister through the mother ” ( وله أخ أو أخت لأم ). All these shares are distributed “after any bequest made or debt,” on condition that no harm is caused ( غَيْرَ مُضَارٍّ ) through bequest or debt—for example, a bequest of more than a third, an unauthorized testamentary allocation to an heir, or a contrivance designed to defeat others’ rights. Where the intention to inflict harm is known, any excess above a third is set aside; the jurists disagreed, in two reported opinions, whether the third itself should also be invalidated. This is Ibn Juzayy’s account. “A bequest from God” ( وَصِيَّةً مِنَ اللَّهِ ) means “We enjoin this upon you as an injunction,” or means “without violating an injunction from God.” Ibn ʿAbbās said, “Causing harm in a bequest is one of the grave sins.” God knows what serves His servants’ welfare, apportions wealth accordingly, and is Forbearing ( حَلِيمٌ ): He does not immediately inflict punishment upon those who violate His bounds."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Brethren under the same teacher share spiritual nourishment","html":"<p>Know that brotherhood through the shaykh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأخوة في الشيخ<\/bdi>) is like brotherhood through kinship, for they suckle at one breast and drink one milk. If one of them dies, his brother inherits the spiritual provision (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المدد<\/bdi>) he used to receive from their teacher. Likewise if a disciple turns back [from the path]—which is a kind of death—the influx is diverted to his brother. It resembles water divided among conduits (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القواديس<\/bdi>): if one conduit is blocked, the water flows towards the others. If there are several spiritual brethren, they become partners in that spiritual influx. And God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Know that brotherhood through the shaykh ( الأخوة في الشيخ ) is like brotherhood through kinship, for they suckle at one breast and drink one milk. If one of them dies, his brother inherits the spiritual provision ( المدد ) he used to receive from their teacher. Likewise if a disciple turns back [from the path]—which is a kind of death—the influx is diverted to his brother. It resembles water divided among conduits ( القواديس ): if one conduit is blocked, the water flows towards the others. If there are several spiritual brethren, they become partners in that spiritual influx. And God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Then the Real—exalted is He—warned against transgressing the limits He had established for bequests and inheritances.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Then the Real—exalted is He—warned against transgressing the limits He had established for bequests and inheritances."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":8888,"arabic_characters":5191},{"id":"q4-13-14","q":"4:13–14","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:13–14 — The limits of the revealed law and the bounds of spiritual discipline","source":"Cairo Arabic edition I:477–478","cairo":"I:477–478","source_page_first":477,"source_page_last":478,"ar":"تِلْكَ حُدُودُ اللَّهِ وَمَنْ يُطِعِ اللَّهَ وَرَسُولَهُ يُدْخِلْهُ جَنَّاتٍ تَجْرِي مِنْ تَحْتِهَا الْأَنْهارُ خالِدِينَ فِيها وَذلِكَ الْفَوْزُ الْعَظِيمُ (13) وَمَنْ يَعْصِ اللَّهَ وَرَسُولَهُ وَيَتَعَدَّ حُدُودَهُ يُدْخِلْهُ نارًا خالِداً فِيها وَلَهُ عَذابٌ مُهِينٌ (14)\nقلت: توحيد الضمير في (نُدخله) (١) مراعاة للفظ (من) . وجمع الحال في (خالدين) مراعاة للمعنى.\nو (خالدين) و (خالدًا) : حال مقدرة من ضمير (نُدخله) ، كقولك: مررت برجل معه صقر صائداً به غدًا، وليسا صفتين لجنات ونارًا، وإلا لوجب إبراز الضمير لأنهما جرتا على غير مَنْ هما له.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: تِلْكَ الأحكام التي شرعناها لكم في أمر الوصايا والمواريث، هي حُدُودُ اللَّهِ حدَّها لكم لتقفوا معها ولا تتعدوها وَمَنْ يُطِعِ اللَّهَ فيما أمر به وحدّه، وَرَسُولَهُ فيما شرَّعه وسنّه نُدْخِلْهُ جَنَّاتٍ تَجْرِي مِنْ تَحْتِهَا الْأَنْهارُ خالِدِينَ فِيها. وَذلِكَ هو الْفَوْزُ أي: الفلاح الْعَظِيمُ، وَمَنْ يَعْصِ اللَّهَ فيما أمر ونهى، وَرَسُولَهُ فيما شرعه، وَيَتَعَدَّ حُدُودَهُ التي حدها، فتجاوز إلى متابعة هواه، نُدْخِلْهُ نارًا خالِداً فِيها وَلَهُ عَذابٌ مُهِينٌ. وهذا إذا أنكر مشروعيتها فيكون كافرًا، وإلاَّ كان عاصيًا في حكم المشيئة، ومذهب أهل السنة أنه لا يخلد، وحملوا الآية على الكافر، أو عبارة عن طول المدة، كما في قاتل النفس. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: قد حدَّ الحق- جل جلاله- لأهل الشريعة الظاهرة حدودًا قام ببيانها العلماء، وحدَّ لأهل الحقيقة- وهي سر الولاية- حدودًا، قام بها الأولياء، فمن قام بحدود الشريعة الظاهرة كان من المؤمنين الصالحين، ومن تعداها كان من العاصين الظالمين، ومن قام بحدود الحقيقة الباطنية، وصحب أهلها كان من المحسنين العارفين المقربين، ودخل جنة المعارف، ومن تعدَّ حدود الحقيقة، أو لم يصحب أهلها كان من عوام أهل اليمين، وله عذاب الحجاب في غم الحساب، وقال في الحاشية: في حد حدوده إشارة للعبودية، في إخراج كل عن نظره واختياره، ثم انقياده وذلته لحكم ربه، والوقوف عند حدوده.\nوقال الورتجبي: قيل: (تلك حدود الله) أي: الإظهار من الأحوال للمريدين على حسب طاقتهم لها، فإن التعدي فيها يهلكهم، وقال أبو عثمان: ما هلك امرؤ لزم حده ولم يتعد طوره. هـ. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nولمّا فرغ الحق تعالى من الأمر بحفظ الأموال، شرع فى الكلام على حفظ الأنساب، وقدّم الكلام أولا على الزنى إذ به تختلط الأنساب، ويختل نظام حفظها، ثم تكلم بعد على النكاح وما يحرم من النساء وما يحل، فقال:","quran_ar":"تِلْكَ حُدُودُ اللَّهِ وَمَنْ يُطِعِ اللَّهَ وَرَسُولَهُ يُدْخِلْهُ جَنَّاتٍ تَجْرِي مِنْ تَحْتِهَا الْأَنْهارُ خالِدِينَ فِيها وَذلِكَ الْفَوْزُ الْعَظِيمُ (13) وَمَنْ يَعْصِ اللَّهَ وَرَسُولَهُ وَيَتَعَدَّ حُدُودَهُ يُدْخِلْهُ نارًا خالِداً فِيها وَلَهُ عَذابٌ مُهِينٌ (14)","quran_en":"13. Those are God’s limits. Whoever obeys God and His Messenger, He will admit to gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein; and that is the great triumph. 14. But whoever disobeys God and His Messenger and transgresses His limits, He will admit to a fire, abiding therein, and his will be a humiliating punishment.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Agreement with man (مَن) and prospective circumstantial qualifiers","html":"<p>I say: The singular pronoun in “We admit him” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نُدْخِلْهُ<\/bdi>) observes the grammatical form of “whoever” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مَنْ<\/bdi>). The plural circumstantial form “abiding” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خَالِدِينَ<\/bdi>) observes its sense, since it includes a multitude of persons. The forms “abiding” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خَالِدِينَ<\/bdi>) and “abiding” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خَالِدًا<\/bdi>) are prospective circumstantial qualifiers (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال مقدرة<\/bdi>) of the object pronoun in “We admit him,” like saying, “I passed a man with a falcon, who will be hunting with it tomorrow.” They are not adjectives of “gardens” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جَنَّات<\/bdi>) and “fire” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نَارًا<\/bdi>): were they adjectives predicated of something other than their grammatical possessor, the pronoun would have to be made explicit. [Reading note: the Cairo verse headings print <em>yudkhilhu<\/em>, “He admits him,” while this grammatical discussion and the following exposition quote <em>nudkhilhu<\/em>, “We admit him.” The Cairo editor attributes the nūn reading to Nāfiʿ and Ibn ʿĀmir, and the yāʾ reading to the other readers. The verse translation follows its displayed heading; the translations of the grammatical discussion and exposition follow the forms they actually quote.]<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: The singular pronoun in “We admit him” ( نُدْخِلْهُ ) observes the grammatical form of “whoever” ( مَنْ ). The plural circumstantial form “abiding” ( خَالِدِينَ ) observes its sense, since it includes a multitude of persons. The forms “abiding” ( خَالِدِينَ ) and “abiding” ( خَالِدًا ) are prospective circumstantial qualifiers ( حال مقدرة ) of the object pronoun in “We admit him,” like saying, “I passed a man with a falcon, who will be hunting with it tomorrow.” They are not adjectives of “gardens” ( جَنَّات ) and “fire” ( نَارًا ): were they adjectives predicated of something other than their grammatical possessor, the pronoun would have to be made explicit. [Reading note: the Cairo verse headings print yudkhilhu , “He admits him,” while this grammatical discussion and the following exposition quote nudkhilhu , “We admit him.” The Cairo editor attributes the nūn reading to Nāfiʿ and Ibn ʿĀmir, and the yāʾ reading to the other readers. The verse translation follows its displayed heading; the translations of the grammatical discussion and exposition follow the forms they actually quote.]"},{"type":"theology","title":"Obedience, disobedience, and the duration of punishment","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—says: The ordinances We have established for you concerning bequests and inheritance “are the limits of God” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حُدُودُ اللَّهِ<\/bdi>), which He marked out for you so that you should stand by them and not transgress them. “Whoever obeys God” in what He commands and limits, “and His Messenger” in what he legislates and establishes by his Sunna, “We will admit him to gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein.” “That is the great triumph” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الْفَوْزُ الْعَظِيمُ<\/bdi>), the supreme success. But “whoever disobeys God” in what He commands or forbids, “and His Messenger” in what he legislates, “and transgresses His limits,” departing from those bounds to pursue desire, “We will admit him to a fire, abiding therein; and for him is a humiliating punishment.” This everlasting punishment applies where the transgressor rejects the legitimacy of the injunctions and thereby becomes an unbeliever. Otherwise he is a sinner subject to the divine will (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المشيئة<\/bdi>). According to the doctrine of the People of the Sunna (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أهل السنة<\/bdi>), he does not remain eternally in the Fire; they interpret the verse as applying to the unbeliever, or take the term to indicate a very long stay, as in the verse concerning the murderer. And God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—says: The ordinances We have established for you concerning bequests and inheritance “are the limits of God” ( حُدُودُ اللَّهِ ), which He marked out for you so that you should stand by them and not transgress them. “Whoever obeys God” in what He commands and limits, “and His Messenger” in what he legislates and establishes by his Sunna, “We will admit him to gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein.” “That is the great triumph” ( الْفَوْزُ الْعَظِيمُ ), the supreme success. But “whoever disobeys God” in what He commands or forbids, “and His Messenger” in what he legislates, “and transgresses His limits,” departing from those bounds to pursue desire, “We will admit him to a fire, abiding therein; and for him is a humiliating punishment.” This everlasting punishment applies where the transgressor rejects the legitimacy of the injunctions and thereby becomes an unbeliever. Otherwise he is a sinner subject to the divine will ( المشيئة ). According to the doctrine of the People of the Sunna ( أهل السنة ), he does not remain eternally in the Fire; they interpret the verse as applying to the unbeliever, or take the term to indicate a very long stay, as in the verse concerning the murderer. And God knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The limits of outward law and of inner spiritual realization","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—has established limits for the people of the outward Law (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشريعة الظاهرة<\/bdi>), which the scholars (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العلماء<\/bdi>) have undertaken to clarify. He has likewise established limits for the people of inward Reality (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحقيقة<\/bdi>), which is the secret of sainthood (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سر الولاية<\/bdi>), and the Friends of God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأولياء<\/bdi>) undertake to maintain them. Whoever observes the limits of outward Law belongs among the righteous believers; whoever exceeds them belongs among sinners and wrongdoers. Whoever observes the bounds of inward Reality and keeps company with its people belongs among the spiritually excellent, the knowers drawn near (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المحسنين العارفين المقربين<\/bdi>), and enters the Garden of gnoses (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جنة المعارف<\/bdi>). Whoever transgresses the bounds of Reality, or fails to accompany its people, belongs to the ordinary People of the Right (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عوام أهل اليمين<\/bdi>) and suffers the punishment of veiling (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عذاب الحجاب<\/bdi>) amid the distress of reckoning. The marginal commentary says: In the designation of His limits lies an indication of servanthood (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العبودية<\/bdi>): removing every person from self-regard and autonomous choosing, then submitting with humility to the judgment of his Lord and stopping at His bounds. Al-Wartajbī says: It has been said that “those are God’s limits” means the states which are made manifest to seekers (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المريدين<\/bdi>) according to their capacity to bear them; to overstep those capacities destroys them. Abū ʿUthmān said: “No one has perished who kept to his limit and did not exceed his station.” Here ends the quotation. God grants success and guides to the even path.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—has established limits for the people of the outward Law ( الشريعة الظاهرة ), which the scholars ( العلماء ) have undertaken to clarify. He has likewise established limits for the people of inward Reality ( الحقيقة ), which is the secret of sainthood ( سر الولاية ), and the Friends of God ( الأولياء ) undertake to maintain them. Whoever observes the limits of outward Law belongs among the righteous believers; whoever exceeds them belongs among sinners and wrongdoers. Whoever observes the bounds of inward Reality and keeps company with its people belongs among the spiritually excellent, the knowers drawn near ( المحسنين العارفين المقربين ), and enters the Garden of gnoses ( جنة المعارف ). Whoever transgresses the bounds of Reality, or fails to accompany its people, belongs to the ordinary People of the Right ( عوام أهل اليمين ) and suffers the punishment of veiling ( عذاب الحجاب ) amid the distress of reckoning. The marginal commentary says: In the designation of His limits lies an indication of servanthood ( العبودية ): removing every person from self-regard and autonomous choosing, then submitting with humility to the judgment of his Lord and stopping at His bounds. Al-Wartajbī says: It has been said that “those are God’s limits” means the states which are made manifest to seekers ( المريدين ) according to their capacity to bear them; to overstep those capacities destroys them. Abū ʿUthmān said: “No one has perished who kept to his limit and did not exceed his station.” Here ends the quotation. God grants success and guides to the even path."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Having completed the command to preserve property, the Real now begins His discussion of the preservation of lineage (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حفظ الأنساب<\/bdi>). He first treats illicit intercourse because it confuses descent and disrupts its protection, then speaks of marriage and of those women who are prohibited or permitted in it.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Having completed the command to preserve property, the Real now begins His discussion of the preservation of lineage ( حفظ الأنساب ). He first treats illicit intercourse because it confuses descent and disrupts its protection, then speaks of marriage and of those women who are prohibited or permitted in it."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C477.1","page":477,"ar":"قرأ نافع وابن عامر «ندخله» بالنون، وقرأ الآخرون بالياء.","en":"Nāfiʿ and Ibn ʿĀmir read nudkhilhu with nūn; the other readers read it with yāʾ.","anchor":"توحيد الضمير في (نُدخله)"}],"english_characters":4280,"arabic_characters":2177},{"id":"q4-15-16","q":"4:15–16","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:15–16 — Early discipline for sexual offences and the purification of the self","source":"Cairo Arabic edition I:478–479","cairo":"I:478–479","source_page_first":478,"source_page_last":479,"ar":"وَاللاَّتِي يَأْتِينَ الْفاحِشَةَ مِنْ نِسائِكُمْ فَاسْتَشْهِدُوا عَلَيْهِنَّ أَرْبَعَةً مِنْكُمْ فَإِنْ شَهِدُوا فَأَمْسِكُوهُنَّ فِي الْبُيُوتِ حَتَّى يَتَوَفَّاهُنَّ الْمَوْتُ أَوْ يَجْعَلَ اللَّهُ لَهُنَّ سَبِيلاً (15) وَالَّذانِ يَأْتِيانِها مِنْكُمْ فَآذُوهُما فَإِنْ تابا وَأَصْلَحا فَأَعْرِضُوا عَنْهُما إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ تَوَّاباً رَحِيماً (16)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَالنساء اللَّاتِي يَأْتِينَ الْفاحِشَةَ أي: الزنى، سُمِّيَ فاحشة لفُحش قبحه وبشاعة فعله شرعًا، مِنْ نِسائِكُمْ المسلمات، فَاسْتَشْهِدُوا عَلَيْهِنَّ أي: اطلبوا مَنْ رَمَاهُنَّ بذلك أن يُشْهِدوا\nعَلَيْهِنَّ أَرْبَعَةً مِنْكُمْ، أي: من عدول المؤمنين يرونهما كالمرود في المكحلة، وإنما جعلوا أربعة مبالغة في الستر على المؤمن، أو ليكون على كل واحد اثنان، فَإِنْ شَهِدُوا عليهن بذلك فَأَمْسِكُوهُنَّ فِي الْبُيُوتِ، واجعلوه سجنًا لهن حَتَّى يَتَوَفَّاهُنَّ الْمَوْتُ أي: يستوفي أجلّهن الموتُ، أو يتوفاهن ملك الموت، أَوْ يَجْعَلَ اللَّهُ لَهُنَّ سَبِيلًا كتعيين الحد المخلّص من السجن، وكان هذا في أول الإسلام ثم نسخ بما في سورة النور من الحدود، ويحتمل أن يراد التوصية بإمساكهن بعد أن يُجلدن كي لا يَعُدْن إلى الزنى بسبب الخروج والتعرض للرجال.\nواكتفى بذكر حدِّهن، بما في سورة النور، وهذا الإمساك كان خاصًا بالنساء بدليل قوله وَالَّذانِ يَأْتِيانِها مِنْكُمْ أي: الزاني والزانية منكم، (فآذوهما) بالتوبيخ والتقريع- (فإن تابا وأصلحا فأعرضوا عنهما) أي:\nاقطعوا عنهما الأذى، أو أعرِضوا عنهما بالإغماض عن ذكر مساوئهما.\nقيل: إن هذه الآية سابقة على الأولى نزولاً، وكان عقوبة الزنى الأذى ثم الحبس ثم الجلد، وقيل، الحبس في المساحقات، والإيذاء في اللواطين، وما في سورة النور في الزناة. والذي يظهر. أن الحكم كان في أول الإسلام في الزنا: الإمساك للنساء في البيوت بعد الإيذاء بالتوبيخ، فتُمسك في بيتها حتى تموت، أو يجعل الله لها سبيلاً بالتزوج بمن يعفها عنه. والإيذاء للرجال بالتعيير والتقريع والتحجيم حتى تتحقق توبته، ثم نسخ ذلك كله بالحدود، وهو جَلْد البكر مائة وتغريبه عامًا ورجم المحصن. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: ينبغي للعبد، إذا طَغَتْ عليه نفسُه، وأرادت ارتكاب الفواحش، أن يستشهد عليها الحفظة، الذين يحفظون عليه تلك المعاصي، فإن لم تستحِ، فليعاقبها بالحبس في سجن الجوع والخلوة والصمت، حتى تموت عن تلك الشهوات، أو يجعل الله لها طريقًا بالوصول إلى شيخ يُغيِّبه عنها، أو بوارد قوي مِن خوف مُزعج أو شوق مقلق، فإن تابت وأصلحت، أعرض عنها واشتغل بذكر الله، ثم يغيب عما سواه. وبالله التوفيق.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى وقت التوبة التي تقبل، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَاللاَّتِي يَأْتِينَ الْفاحِشَةَ مِنْ نِسائِكُمْ فَاسْتَشْهِدُوا عَلَيْهِنَّ أَرْبَعَةً مِنْكُمْ فَإِنْ شَهِدُوا فَأَمْسِكُوهُنَّ فِي الْبُيُوتِ حَتَّى يَتَوَفَّاهُنَّ الْمَوْتُ أَوْ يَجْعَلَ اللَّهُ لَهُنَّ سَبِيلاً (15) وَالَّذانِ يَأْتِيانِها مِنْكُمْ فَآذُوهُما فَإِنْ تابا وَأَصْلَحا فَأَعْرِضُوا عَنْهُما إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ تَوَّاباً رَحِيماً (16)","quran_en":"15. As for those women among you who commit an indecency, summon four witnesses from among yourselves to testify against them; if the witnesses testify, confine them within the houses until death takes them or God appoints for them another way. 16. And the two among you who commit it, inflict a penalty upon them; but if they repent and amend themselves, then leave them alone. Truly God is Ever-Relenting, Merciful.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Four witnesses, seclusion and subsequent penal provisions","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—says: “Those women who commit indecency” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الْفَاحِشَةَ<\/bdi>), that is, unlawful sexual intercourse (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الزنا<\/bdi>), so called because of its extreme moral repulsiveness and the gravity of its prohibition in the Sacred Law, “from among your women”—Muslim women—“summon four witnesses to testify against them.” Require those who accuse them to produce four upright believers who witnessed the act unmistakably, as [the classical legal simile says] the kohl-stick entering its container. Four witnesses are required as an exceptional protection of the believer’s reputation and privacy, or so that the proof may consist of two witnesses for each party. “If they testify” to that effect, “then confine the women in houses”: make the houses their places of imprisonment “until death takes them,” completing their lifespan, or the Angel of Death takes them, “or God appoints some way for them” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سَبِيلًا<\/bdi>), such as a specified fixed penalty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حدّ<\/bdi>) which releases them from confinement. That was the early rule of Islam, subsequently abrogated by the prescribed penalties in Sūrat al-Nūr. It is also possible to understand the verse as recommending confinement even after flogging, so that the women do not return to illicit relations through going outside and meeting men. Their fixed penalty is then left to the verse of al-Nūr. Confinement was specifically mentioned for women, as is clear from the following words, “The two who commit it among you,” meaning [on his interpretation] the man and woman who commit fornication: “inflict a penalty upon them,” by admonition and reproach. “If they repent and amend themselves, turn away from them”: stop imposing reproach, or refrain from recalling and disclosing their past wrongdoing. Some say that the second verse was revealed before the first: the succession of punishments for fornication was then admonition, confinement, and finally flogging. Others apply confinement to women engaging in same-sex acts and admonition to men engaging in same-sex acts, leaving the provisions in Sūrat al-Nūr for illicit intercourse. What appears to Ibn ʿAjība to have been the earliest ruling concerning fornication is that women were confined to their homes after admonition until death, or until God provided them a way through marriage to someone who would keep them chaste; and that men were subjected to reproach, public condemnation and [social] restraint until their repentance became manifest. All this was then abrogated by the fixed penalties: one hundred lashes and one year of exile for an unmarried offender, and stoning for an offender previously married in the relevant legal sense (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المحصن<\/bdi>). And God knows best. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N110\">[N110]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—says: “Those women who commit indecency” ( الْفَاحِشَةَ ), that is, unlawful sexual intercourse ( الزنا ), so called because of its extreme moral repulsiveness and the gravity of its prohibition in the Sacred Law, “from among your women”—Muslim women—“summon four witnesses to testify against them.” Require those who accuse them to produce four upright believers who witnessed the act unmistakably, as [the classical legal simile says] the kohl-stick entering its container. Four witnesses are required as an exceptional protection of the believer’s reputation and privacy, or so that the proof may consist of two witnesses for each party. “If they testify” to that effect, “then confine the women in houses”: make the houses their places of imprisonment “until death takes them,” completing their lifespan, or the Angel of Death takes them, “or God appoints some way for them” ( سَبِيلًا ), such as a specified fixed penalty ( حدّ ) which releases them from confinement. That was the early rule of Islam, subsequently abrogated by the prescribed penalties in Sūrat al-Nūr. It is also possible to understand the verse as recommending confinement even after flogging, so that the women do not return to illicit relations through going outside and meeting men. Their fixed penalty is then left to the verse of al-Nūr. Confinement was specifically mentioned for women, as is clear from the following words, “The two who commit it among you,” meaning [on his interpretation] the man and woman who commit fornication: “inflict a penalty upon them,” by admonition and reproach. “If they repent and amend themselves, turn away from them”: stop imposing reproach, or refrain from recalling and disclosing their past wrongdoing. Some say that the second verse was revealed before the first: the succession of punishments for fornication was then admonition, confinement, and finally flogging. Others apply confinement to women engaging in same-sex acts and admonition to men engaging in same-sex acts, leaving the provisions in Sūrat al-Nūr for illicit intercourse. What appears to Ibn ʿAjība to have been the earliest ruling concerning fornication is that women were confined to their homes after admonition until death, or until God provided them a way through marriage to someone who would keep them chaste; and that men were subjected to reproach, public condemnation and [social] restraint until their repentance became manifest. All this was then abrogated by the fixed penalties: one hundred lashes and one year of exile for an unmarried offender, and stoning for an offender previously married in the relevant legal sense ( المحصن ). And God knows best. [N110]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Confine destructive desires, not the body itself","html":"<p>When the servant’s self (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النفس<\/bdi>) grows rebellious and wishes to perpetrate indecencies, he should call upon the recording guardians (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحفظة<\/bdi>)—those who register his misdeeds—to testify against it. If it is still unashamed, he should discipline it by confining it in the prison of hunger, spiritual seclusion (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخلوة<\/bdi>) and silence until it dies to those desires, or until God creates a path for it through the servant’s reaching a shaykh who makes him absent from his self, or by a powerful spiritual arrival (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وارد<\/bdi>) of disturbing fear or unsettling longing. If the self repents and is reformed, he should cease his reproach of it and occupy himself in remembrance of God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الذكر<\/bdi>), until he is absent from everything besides Him. And God grants success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"When the servant’s self ( النفس ) grows rebellious and wishes to perpetrate indecencies, he should call upon the recording guardians ( الحفظة )—those who register his misdeeds—to testify against it. If it is still unashamed, he should discipline it by confining it in the prison of hunger, spiritual seclusion ( الخلوة ) and silence until it dies to those desires, or until God creates a path for it through the servant’s reaching a shaykh who makes him absent from his self, or by a powerful spiritual arrival ( وارد ) of disturbing fear or unsettling longing. If the self repents and is reformed, he should cease his reproach of it and occupy himself in remembrance of God ( الذكر ), until he is absent from everything besides Him. And God grants success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Then the Real mentions the time at which repentance is accepted.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Then the Real mentions the time at which repentance is accepted."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3512,"arabic_characters":2290},{"id":"q4-17-18","q":"4:17–18","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:17–18 — Repentance before death and the different accountability of ordinary and advanced seekers","source":"Cairo Arabic edition I:479–481","cairo":"I:479–481","source_page_first":479,"source_page_last":481,"ar":"إِنَّمَا التَّوْبَةُ عَلَى اللَّهِ لِلَّذِينَ يَعْمَلُونَ السُّوءَ بِجَهالَةٍ ثُمَّ يَتُوبُونَ مِنْ قَرِيبٍ فَأُولئِكَ يَتُوبُ اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلِيماً حَكِيماً (17) وَلَيْسَتِ التَّوْبَةُ لِلَّذِينَ يَعْمَلُونَ السَّيِّئاتِ حَتَّى إِذا حَضَرَ أَحَدَهُمُ الْمَوْتُ قالَ إِنِّي تُبْتُ الْآنَ وَلا الَّذِينَ يَمُوتُونَ وَهُمْ كُفَّارٌ أُولئِكَ أَعْتَدْنا لَهُمْ عَذاباً أَلِيماً (18)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّمَا التَّوْبَةُ التي يُستحق عَلَى اللَّهِ قبولُها فضلاً وإحسانًا هي لِلَّذِينَ يَعْمَلُونَ السُّوءَ أي: المعاصي متلبسين بِجَهالَةٍ أي: سفاهة وجهل وسوء أدب، فكل من اشتغل بالمعصية فهو جاهل بالله، قد انتزع منه الإيمان حتى يفرغ، وإن كان عالمًا بكونها معصية، ثُمَّ يَتُوبُونَ بعد تلك المعصية مِنْ قَرِيبٍ أي: من زمن قريب، وهو قبل حضور الموت لقوله بعدُ: حَتَّى إِذا حَضَرَ أَحَدَهُمُ الْمَوْتُ، وقوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «إن الله يقبلُ توبةَّ العبْدِ مَا لمْ يُغَرْغِرُ» وإنما جعله قريبًا لأن الدنيا سريعة الزوال، متاعها قليل وزمانها قريب، فَأُولئِكَ يَتُوبُ اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْ تصديقاً لوعده المتقدم، وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلِيماً بإخلاصهم التوبة، حَكِيماً في ترك معاقبة التائب، إذ الحكمة هي وضع الشيء في محله.\nوعن الحسن: قال: قال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «لما أُهبط إبليسُ قال: وعزتك وعظمتك لا أفارق ابنَ آدم حتى تفارقَ روحُه جسدَه، قال الله تعالى: وعزتي وعظمتي لا أحجب التوبة عن عبدي حتى يغرغر بها» . وعن أبي سعيد الخدري أن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم قال: «إنّ الشيطانَ قال: وعزتِك لا أبرح أُغوى عبادكَ، ما دامتْ أرواحهم في أجسادهم. قال الله تعالى: وعزتي وجلالي وارتفاع مكاني لا أزال أغفر لهم ما استغفروني» .\nقال ابن جزي: وإذا تاب العبد توبة صحيحة بشروطها، فيقطع بقبول توبته عند جمهور العلماء. وقال أبو المعالي: يغلب ذلك على الظن ولا يقطع. هـ.\nوَلَيْسَتِ التَّوْبَةُ مقبولة لِلَّذِينَ يَعْمَلُونَ السَّيِّئاتِ حَتَّى إِذا حَضَرَ أَحَدَهُمُ الْمَوْتُ أي: بلغت الحلقوم قالَ إِنِّي تُبْتُ الْآنَ وَلَا الَّذِينَ يَمُوتُونَ وَهُمْ كُفَّارٌ فلا توبة لهم، أُولئِكَ أَعْتَدْنا أي: أعددنا وهيأنا لَهُمْ عَذاباً أَلِيماً، قال البيضاوي: سوَّى الحقُ تعالى بين من سوَّف التوبة إلى حضور الموت من الفسقة، وبين مَن مات على الكفر في نفي التوبة للمبالغة في عدم الاعتداد بها في تلك الحالة، وكأنه يقول: توبة هؤلاء وعدم توبة هؤلاء سواء. وقيل: المراد بالذين يعملون السوء: عصاة المؤمنين، وبالذين يعملون السيئات: المنافقون لتضاعف كفرهم، وبالذين يموتون: الكفار. هـ.\nالإشارة: توبة العوام ليست كتوبة الخواص، إنَّ الله يمهل العوام ترغيبًا لهم في الرجوع، ويُعاقب الخواص على التأخير على قدر مقامهم في القرب من الحضرة، فكلما عظُم القربُ عظمت المحاسبة على ترك المراقبة، منهم من يسامح له في لحظة، ومنهم في ساعة، ومنهم في ساعتين، على قدر المقام، ثم يُعاتبهم ويردهم إلى الحضرة.\nوقال شيخ شيوخنا سيدي عبد الرحمن الفاسي في حاشيته: إِنَّمَا التَّوْبَةُ عَلَى اللَّهِ أي: إنما الهداية بعد الذلة، على الله لأنه الذي يُخلص من قَهْره بكرمه الفياض وبرحمته التي غلبت غضبه، كما قال تعالى:\nكَتَبَ رَبُّكُمْ عَلى نَفْسِهِ الرَّحْمَةَ، ونبه على وقوع الذنب بهم قهرًا، ثم تداركهم بالهداية والإنابة، فضلاً على علمه بتربيتهم وتدريجهم لمعرفته بالعلم والحكمة بقوله: وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلِيماً حَكِيماً. هـ.\nثم شرع فى أحكام النكاح، وبدأ بالعضل لأنه يتعذر معه العقد، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنَّمَا التَّوْبَةُ عَلَى اللَّهِ لِلَّذِينَ يَعْمَلُونَ السُّوءَ بِجَهالَةٍ ثُمَّ يَتُوبُونَ مِنْ قَرِيبٍ فَأُولئِكَ يَتُوبُ اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلِيماً حَكِيماً (17) وَلَيْسَتِ التَّوْبَةُ لِلَّذِينَ يَعْمَلُونَ السَّيِّئاتِ حَتَّى إِذا حَضَرَ أَحَدَهُمُ الْمَوْتُ قالَ إِنِّي تُبْتُ الْآنَ وَلا الَّذِينَ يَمُوتُونَ وَهُمْ كُفَّارٌ أُولئِكَ أَعْتَدْنا لَهُمْ عَذاباً أَلِيماً (18)","quran_en":"17. God’s acceptance of repentance is for those who do wrong in ignorance and then repent soon afterwards; these are the ones to whom God turns in mercy. And God is Knowing, Wise. 18. But repentance is not for those who persist in wrongdoing until, when death comes to one of them, he says, “Now I repent,” nor for those who die while denying the truth. For these We have prepared a painful punishment.","segments":[{"type":"theology","title":"Wrongdoing in ignorance and the point at which repentance is barred","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—says: “God’s acceptance of repentance” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إِنَّمَا التَّوْبَةُ عَلَى اللَّهِ<\/bdi>)—the repentance whose acceptance God has graciously undertaken, as a favor and kindness—is for those who do evil, that is, acts of disobedience, “in ignorance” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بِجَهَالَةٍ<\/bdi>), in the sense of foolishness, lack of discernment, and failure in proper conduct. Everyone engaged in wrongdoing is in that moment ignorant of God; faith has been withdrawn from him until he ceases, even if he knows that what he does is a sin. “Then they repent soon afterward” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مِنْ قَرِيبٍ<\/bdi>), meaning before the onset of death, as clarified by the later words “until death comes to one of them.” The Prophet said, “God accepts the repentance of the servant so long as he has not begun to gasp at death” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إِنَّ اللَّهَ يَقْبَلُ تَوْبَةَ الْعَبْدِ مَا لَمْ يُغَرْغِرْ<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H013\">[H013]<\/a> The verse describes that interval as “near” because this world passes swiftly: its goods are few and its lifetime short. “Those are the ones to whom God turns in mercy,” fulfilling His earlier promise; He knows the sincerity of their repentance and is wise in not punishing the repentant, for wisdom is to put each thing in its right place. A narration attributed through al-Ḥasan says that the Prophet reported: When Iblīs was brought down, he said, “By Your might and majesty, I shall not leave the son of Adam until his spirit leaves his body.” God said: “By My might and majesty, I shall not withhold repentance from My servant until he reaches the death-rattle.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H014\">[H014]<\/a> And from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, the Prophet is reported to have said: “The Devil said, ‘By Your might, I shall not cease misleading Your servants as long as their souls are in their bodies.’ God—exalted is He—said, ‘By My might and majesty and the loftiness of My station, I shall continue forgiving them so long as they seek forgiveness from Me.’” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H015\">[H015]<\/a> Ibn Juzayy says: When the servant repents with genuine repentance, fulfilling its conditions, most scholars hold that he can be certain of its acceptance. Abū al-Maʿālī held instead that acceptance is strongly probable, rather than certain. Here ends the quotation.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—says: “God’s acceptance of repentance” ( إِنَّمَا التَّوْبَةُ عَلَى اللَّهِ )—the repentance whose acceptance God has graciously undertaken, as a favor and kindness—is for those who do evil, that is, acts of disobedience, “in ignorance” ( بِجَهَالَةٍ ), in the sense of foolishness, lack of discernment, and failure in proper conduct. Everyone engaged in wrongdoing is in that moment ignorant of God; faith has been withdrawn from him until he ceases, even if he knows that what he does is a sin. “Then they repent soon afterward” ( مِنْ قَرِيبٍ ), meaning before the onset of death, as clarified by the later words “until death comes to one of them.” The Prophet said, “God accepts the repentance of the servant so long as he has not begun to gasp at death” ( إِنَّ اللَّهَ يَقْبَلُ تَوْبَةَ الْعَبْدِ مَا لَمْ يُغَرْغِرْ ). [H013] The verse describes that interval as “near” because this world passes swiftly: its goods are few and its lifetime short. “Those are the ones to whom God turns in mercy,” fulfilling His earlier promise; He knows the sincerity of their repentance and is wise in not punishing the repentant, for wisdom is to put each thing in its right place. A narration attributed through al-Ḥasan says that the Prophet reported: When Iblīs was brought down, he said, “By Your might and majesty, I shall not leave the son of Adam until his spirit leaves his body.” God said: “By My might and majesty, I shall not withhold repentance from My servant until he reaches the death-rattle.” [H014] And from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, the Prophet is reported to have said: “The Devil said, ‘By Your might, I shall not cease misleading Your servants as long as their souls are in their bodies.’ God—exalted is He—said, ‘By My might and majesty and the loftiness of My station, I shall continue forgiving them so long as they seek forgiveness from Me.’” [H015] Ibn Juzayy says: When the servant repents with genuine repentance, fulfilling its conditions, most scholars hold that he can be certain of its acceptance. Abū al-Maʿālī held instead that acceptance is strongly probable, rather than certain. Here ends the quotation."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Repentance deferred until the death-rattle","html":"<p>“Repentance is not accepted” for those who persist in sinful deeds “until, when death comes to one of them,” that is, when it reaches his throat, “he says, ‘Now I have repented,’” nor for those who “die while unbelievers.” For these no repentance remains; “We have prepared for them a painful punishment.” Al-Bayḍāwī says: God equates those who delay repentance until the presence of death, although sinful believers, with those who die in unbelief, in negating the acceptance of repentance, to emphasize how little such last-minute repentance counts at that point—as though the one’s repentance and the other’s lack of it are alike. Another interpretation understands those who do “wrong” as disobedient believers, those who persist in “evil deeds” as hypocrites on account of the doubling of their unbelief, and those who die as unbelievers as unbelievers openly so. Here ends the quotation.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"“Repentance is not accepted” for those who persist in sinful deeds “until, when death comes to one of them,” that is, when it reaches his throat, “he says, ‘Now I have repented,’” nor for those who “die while unbelievers.” For these no repentance remains; “We have prepared for them a painful punishment.” Al-Bayḍāwī says: God equates those who delay repentance until the presence of death, although sinful believers, with those who die in unbelief, in negating the acceptance of repentance, to emphasize how little such last-minute repentance counts at that point—as though the one’s repentance and the other’s lack of it are alike. Another interpretation understands those who do “wrong” as disobedient believers, those who persist in “evil deeds” as hypocrites on account of the doubling of their unbelief, and those who die as unbelievers as unbelievers openly so. Here ends the quotation."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Repentance in proportion to one’s degree of nearness","html":"<p>Repentance among ordinary people (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">توبة العوام<\/bdi>) is not like repentance among the elect (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">توبة الخواص<\/bdi>). God grants respite to the ordinary to encourage them to return, but calls the elect to account for delay in proportion to their station of proximity to the divine Presence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحضرة<\/bdi>). The greater the nearness, the greater the reckoning for neglecting watchfulness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المراقبة<\/bdi>): some are indulged for an instant, others for an hour, still others for two hours, according to their station; then they are reproached and returned to the Presence. Our masters’ master, Sīdī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Fāsī, says in his marginal commentary concerning “repentance is upon God” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إِنَّمَا التَّوْبَةُ عَلَى اللَّهِ<\/bdi>): This means that guidance after abasement belongs to God, for it is He who releases people from His irresistible power (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قهر<\/bdi>) through abundant generosity and through His mercy that prevails over His wrath, as He says, “Your Lord has prescribed mercy upon Himself” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كَتَبَ رَبُّكُمْ عَلَى نَفْسِهِ الرَّحْمَةَ<\/bdi>) [Q 6:54]. The expression draws attention to how sin overtakes them under overpowering circumstances, after which He reaches them with guidance and penitence, as a grace arising from His knowledge of how to educate them and bring them by degrees into knowing Him. Hence His words, “God is Knowing, Wise.” Here ends the note.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Repentance among ordinary people ( توبة العوام ) is not like repentance among the elect ( توبة الخواص ). God grants respite to the ordinary to encourage them to return, but calls the elect to account for delay in proportion to their station of proximity to the divine Presence ( الحضرة ). The greater the nearness, the greater the reckoning for neglecting watchfulness ( المراقبة ): some are indulged for an instant, others for an hour, still others for two hours, according to their station; then they are reproached and returned to the Presence. Our masters’ master, Sīdī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Fāsī, says in his marginal commentary concerning “repentance is upon God” ( إِنَّمَا التَّوْبَةُ عَلَى اللَّهِ ): This means that guidance after abasement belongs to God, for it is He who releases people from His irresistible power ( قهر ) through abundant generosity and through His mercy that prevails over His wrath, as He says, “Your Lord has prescribed mercy upon Himself” ( كَتَبَ رَبُّكُمْ عَلَى نَفْسِهِ الرَّحْمَةَ ) [Q 6:54]. The expression draws attention to how sin overtakes them under overpowering circumstances, after which He reaches them with guidance and penitence, as a grace arising from His knowledge of how to educate them and bring them by degrees into knowing Him. Hence His words, “God is Knowing, Wise.” Here ends the note."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then begins the rules governing marriage, starting with unjust obstruction (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العَضْل<\/bdi>), because it prevents the contract from being concluded.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then begins the rules governing marriage, starting with unjust obstruction ( العَضْل ), because it prevents the contract from being concluded."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4537,"arabic_characters":3031},{"id":"q4-19","q":"4:19","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:19 — Women’s rights in marriage; reciprocal kindness and gentleness toward the disciplined self","source":"Cairo Arabic edition I:481–483","cairo":"I:481–483","source_page_first":481,"source_page_last":483,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا يَحِلُّ لَكُمْ أَنْ تَرِثُوا النِّساءَ كَرْهاً وَلا تَعْضُلُوهُنَّ لِتَذْهَبُوا بِبَعْضِ مَآ آتَيْتُمُوهُنَّ إِلاَّ أَنْ يَأْتِينَ بِفاحِشَةٍ مُبَيِّنَةٍ ...\nقلت: أصل العضل: التضييق، يقال: عضلَت الدجاجة ببيضها إذا ضاقت، ثم أُطلق عُرفًا على منع المرأة من التزوج.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا يحل لكم أن تمنعوا النساء من النكاح لترثوا ما لهن كَرْهاً. قال ابن عباس: كانوا في الجاهلية إذا مات الرجل، وله امرأة، كان قريبه من عصبته أحق بها من نفسها ومن غيره، فإن شاء تزوجها من غير صداق، إلا الصداق الأول الذي أصدقها الميت، وإن شاء زوّجها غيره وأخذ صداقها ولم يُعطها شيئًا، وإن شاء عَضَلهَا وضيَّق عليها لتفتدى منه بما ورثت من الميت، أو تموت فيرثُها، وإن ذهبت إلى أهلها قبل أن يُلقِيَ وليُّ زوجها ثوبَه عليها فهي أحق بنفسها. فكانوا على ذلك في أول الإسلام، حتى توفي أبو قَيس بن الأَسْلتَ الأنصاري، وترك امرأته «كبشة بنت معن الأنصارية» ، فقام ابنٌ له من غيرها فطرح ثوبه عليها، ثم تركها ولم يقربها، ولم ينفق عليها، يضارّها لتفتدى منه، فأتت إلى النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم فقالت: يا رسول الله إن أبا قيس تُوفي ووَرِثَ نكاحي ابنُه، وقد أضرَّ بي وطوّل عليّ، فلا هو ينفق عليّ ولا يدخل بي، ولا يخلي سبيلي، فقال لها النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «اقعدي في بيتك حتى يأتَي فيك أمرُ الله» . قالت: فانصرفتُ وسمعت بذلك النساءُ في المدينة فأتين النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم وهو في مسجد الفضيخ، فقلن: يا رسول الله: ما نحن إلا كهيئة كبشة، غير أنه لا ينكحنا الأبناء، ونكَحَنا أبناء العَم، فنزلت الآية. فمعنى الآية على هذا: لا يحل لكم أن تجعلوا النساء يُورثن عن الرجال كما يُورث المال.\nوقيل: الخطاب للأزواج الذين يمسكون المرأة في العصمة ليرثوا مالها، من غير غبطة بها، وإنما يمسكها انتظارًا لموتها، وقيل: الخطاب للأولياء الذين يمنعون ولياتهم من التزوج ليرثوهن دون الزوج.\nوَلا يحل لكم أيضًا أيها الأزواج أن تَعْضُلُوهُنَّ، أي: تحبسوهن من غير حاجة لكم فيهن لِتَذْهَبُوا بِبَعْضِ ما آتَيْتُمُوهُنَّ من الصداق افتداء فيه بإضراره. قال ابن عباس رضي الله عنه: (هي أيضًا في الأزواج الذين\nيمسكون المرأة ويُسيئُون عشرتها حتى تفتدى بصداقها) ، إِلَّا أَنْ يَأْتِينَ بِفاحِشَةٍ مُبَيِّنَةٍ، كالنشوز وسوء العشرة وعدم العفة، فيحل له حينئِذ حبسها حتى تفتدى منه بصداقها، فيأخذه خلعًا على مذهب مالك. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: لا يحل للمريد أن يُضيق على نفسه تضييقاً يُفضي إلى العطب، فالنفس كالبهيمة: علفها واستخدامها، وقد قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «لا يكن أحدكم كالمنبت، لا أرضا قطع ولا ظهرًا أبقى» .\nفبعض الناس يسمعون أن من ضيَّق على نفسه أورثته العلوم، فيضيق عليها تضييقًا فاحشًا ليرث ذلك منها كرهًا، وإنما يمنعها من شهواتها الزائدة على قيام البنية، إلا أن تأتي بفاحشة مبينة، بحيث تطغى عليها، فيضيق عليها بما لا يُفضي إلى الهلاك، وهذا كله إنما ينفعه إذا صح مِلْكُه لها بالعقد الصحيح من الشيخ الكامل، وإلاَّ كان تعبه باطلاً، كمن يريد أن يرى امرأة غيره أو دابة غيره. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم أمر الحق تعالى بحسن العشرة مع النساء، فقال:\n... وَعاشِرُوهُنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ فَإِنْ كَرِهْتُمُوهُنَّ فَعَسى أَنْ تَكْرَهُوا شَيْئاً وَيَجْعَلَ اللَّهُ فِيهِ خَيْراً كَثِيراً (19)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وعاشروا النساء بِالْمَعْرُوفِ بأن تلاطفوهن في المقال وتجملوا معهن في الفعال، أو يَتَزَيَّنُ لها كما تتزين له. قال الورتجبي: كونوا في معاشرتهن في مقام الأنس وروح المحبة، وفرح العشق حين أنتم مخصوصون بالتمكين والاستقامة والولاية، فإن معاشرة النساء لا تليق إلا في المستأنس بالله، كالنبى صلّى الله عليه وسلم وجميع المستأنسين من الأولياء والأبدال، حيث أخبر صلّى الله عليه وسلم عن كمال مقام أُنْسِه بالله ورؤيته لجمال مشاهدته حيث قال: «حُبِّبَ إلي من دنياكم ثَلاث: الطيب، والنساء، وجُعلت قرة عيني في الصلاة. (١)»\nثم قال: عن ذي النون: المستأنس بالله يستأنس بكل شيء مليح ووجه صبيح، وبكل صوت طيب وبكل رائحة طيبة. ثم قال: عن ابن المبارك: العشرة الصحيحة: ما لا يورثك الندم عاجلاً ولا آجلاً، وقال أبو حفص: المعاشرة بالمعروف: حسن الخلق مع العيال فيما ساءك. هـ.\nفَإِنْ كَرِهْتُمُوهُنَّ فاصبروا فَعَسى أَنْ تَكْرَهُوا شَيْئاً وَيَجْعَلَ اللَّهُ فِيهِ خَيْراً كَثِيراً إما ولدًا صالحًا أو عاقبة حسنة في الدين. قال ابن عمر: إن الرجل يستخير الله فيخار له فيسخط على ربه، فلا يلبث أن ينظر في العاقبة فإذا هو قد خير له. هـ. حكى أن أبا الإمام مالك رضي الله عنه تزوج امرأة فدخل عليها فوجدها سوداء، فبقي متفكرًا\nولم يقربها، فقالت له: هل استخرت ربك؟ فقال: نعم، فقالت: أتَتَّهمُ ربك، فدخل بها، فحملت بالإمام مالك صاحب المذهب. وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم: «لا يَفْرك مؤمنٌ مؤمنة- أي لا يُبْغِضها- إن سخط منها خُلقَا رضي منها آخر» . قال الورتجبي: قيل: غيب عنك العواقب لئلا تسكن إلى مألوف، ولا تفر من مكروه.\nالإشارة: إذا طهرت النفس من البقايا، وكملت فيها المزايا، وانقادت بكليتها إلى مولاها، وجب الإحسان إليها والصلح معها ومعاشرتها بالمعروف، فإنما تجب مجاهدتها ما دامت كافرة فإذا أسلمت وانقادت وجب محبتها والإحسان إليها. فإن كرهتها في حال اعوجاجها فجاهدتها ورضتها حتى استقامت كان في عاقبة ذلك خيرٌ كثير، وعادت تأتي إليك بالعلوم اللدنية تشاهد فيها أسرارًا ربانية.\nقال الورتجبي: كل أمر من الله- سبحانه- جاء على مخالفة النفس امتحانًا واختبارًا، والنفس كارهة في العبودية فإذا ألزمت عليها حقوق الله بنعت الرياضة والمجاهدة واستقامت في عبودية الله، أول ما يطلع على قلبك أنوار جنان القرب والمشاهدة، قال الله تعالى: وَنَهَى النَّفْسَ عَنِ الْهَوى، فَإِنَّ الْجَنَّةَ هِيَ الْمَأْوى، وفى أجواف ظلام المجاهدة للعارفين شموس المجاهدات وأقمار المكاشفات. هـ. المراد منه.\nفإذا لم يصبر العبد على أذى زوجته، وأراد فراقها، فلا بد أن يؤدى إليها صداقها، كما أشار إلى ذلك الحق جل جلاله، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا يَحِلُّ لَكُمْ أَنْ تَرِثُوا النِّساءَ كَرْهاً وَلا تَعْضُلُوهُنَّ لِتَذْهَبُوا بِبَعْضِ مَآ آتَيْتُمُوهُنَّ إِلاَّ أَنْ يَأْتِينَ بِفاحِشَةٍ مُبَيِّنَةٍ وَعاشِرُوهُنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ فَإِنْ كَرِهْتُمُوهُنَّ فَعَسى أَنْ تَكْرَهُوا شَيْئاً وَيَجْعَلَ اللَّهُ فِيهِ خَيْراً كَثِيراً (19)","quran_en":"19. O believers, it is not lawful for you to inherit women against their will; nor may you constrain them in order to take away part of what you have given them, unless they commit a manifest indecency. Live with them in a manner recognized as good. If you dislike them, it may be that you dislike something in which God has placed much good.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The derivation of ʿaḍl (العَضْل)","html":"<p>I say: The root meaning of <i>ʿaḍl<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العَضْل<\/bdi>) is constraint or making a thing narrow. One says that a hen is <i>ʿaḍilat bi-bayḍihā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عضلت ببيضها<\/bdi>) when it cannot pass its egg because of constriction. In conventional usage the word came to refer to preventing a woman from marrying.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: The root meaning of ʿaḍl ( العَضْل ) is constraint or making a thing narrow. One says that a hen is ʿaḍilat bi-bayḍihā ( عضلت ببيضها ) when it cannot pass its egg because of constriction. In conventional usage the word came to refer to preventing a woman from marrying."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The Qurʾānic prohibition of treating widows as property","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—says: “O believers, it is not lawful for you to prevent women from marrying in order to inherit what they own, against their will.” Ibn ʿAbbās relates that in the age of ignorance, when a man died leaving a wife, a male relative from his agnatic family (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عصبته<\/bdi>) regarded himself as more entitled to her than she was to herself or anyone else. If he wished he married her without a new bridal payment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صداق<\/bdi>), beyond what the deceased had given her. If he wished he married her to another and took her bridal payment for himself; or he impeded and constrained her, so that she would redeem herself with the property inherited from the deceased, or eventually die and leave him her wealth. If she escaped to her family before her deceased husband’s relative cast his garment over her, she was entitled to decide for herself. Ibn ʿAjība relates that this custom persisted into the earliest Islamic period until Abū Qays ibn al-Aslat al-Anṣārī died, leaving his widow Kabsha bint Maʿn al-Anṣāriyya. A son of his from another wife cast his garment over Kabsha and then abandoned her, not consummating a marriage or providing maintenance, so that she might be compelled to purchase her freedom from him. She came to the Prophet and said: “Messenger of God, Abū Qays has died and his son has inherited control of my marriage. He has harmed and delayed me; he neither supports me nor cohabits with me, nor will he let me go.” The Prophet is reported to have replied: “Stay in your house until God makes His command regarding you.” Women in Medina learned of this and came to the Prophet while he was in the Mosque of al-Fuḍaykh, saying: “Our situation is like Kabsha’s, except that it is not sons who marry us but cousins.” Then the verse was revealed. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H016\">[H016]<\/a> On this interpretation the verse means: You are not permitted to treat women as possessions inherited from men as money is inherited. A second explanation addresses husbands who keep wives within the marriage bond solely in hope of inheriting their money when they die, without finding any affection or benefit in the marriage. A third addresses guardians who prevent female wards from marrying to ensure that their wealth comes to the guardian rather than to their eventual husbands.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—says: “O believers, it is not lawful for you to prevent women from marrying in order to inherit what they own, against their will.” Ibn ʿAbbās relates that in the age of ignorance, when a man died leaving a wife, a male relative from his agnatic family ( عصبته ) regarded himself as more entitled to her than she was to herself or anyone else. If he wished he married her without a new bridal payment ( صداق ), beyond what the deceased had given her. If he wished he married her to another and took her bridal payment for himself; or he impeded and constrained her, so that she would redeem herself with the property inherited from the deceased, or eventually die and leave him her wealth. If she escaped to her family before her deceased husband’s relative cast his garment over her, she was entitled to decide for herself. Ibn ʿAjība relates that this custom persisted into the earliest Islamic period until Abū Qays ibn al-Aslat al-Anṣārī died, leaving his widow Kabsha bint Maʿn al-Anṣāriyya. A son of his from another wife cast his garment over Kabsha and then abandoned her, not consummating a marriage or providing maintenance, so that she might be compelled to purchase her freedom from him. She came to the Prophet and said: “Messenger of God, Abū Qays has died and his son has inherited control of my marriage. He has harmed and delayed me; he neither supports me nor cohabits with me, nor will he let me go.” The Prophet is reported to have replied: “Stay in your house until God makes His command regarding you.” Women in Medina learned of this and came to the Prophet while he was in the Mosque of al-Fuḍaykh, saying: “Our situation is like Kabsha’s, except that it is not sons who marry us but cousins.” Then the verse was revealed. [H016] On this interpretation the verse means: You are not permitted to treat women as possessions inherited from men as money is inherited. A second explanation addresses husbands who keep wives within the marriage bond solely in hope of inheriting their money when they die, without finding any affection or benefit in the marriage. A third addresses guardians who prevent female wards from marrying to ensure that their wealth comes to the guardian rather than to their eventual husbands."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Forbidding coercion to recover the dower; the specified exception","html":"<p>Nor is it lawful for husbands to “constrain” their wives, retaining them without wishing to fulfil their marital rights, “so as to take away part of what you gave them,” meaning the bridal gift (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الصداق<\/bdi>), by harming them until they surrender it in return for release. Ibn ʿAbbās says that this, too, concerns husbands who keep their wives while mistreating them until they offer the bridal gift to free themselves. “Unless they commit a manifest indecency” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فَاحِشَةٍ مُبَيِّنَةٍ<\/bdi>), such as rebellious misconduct (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النشوز<\/bdi>), abusive behaviour or unchastity, in which case, on the Mālikī view Ibn ʿAjība records, a husband may constrain her until she redeems herself by a return of the bridal gift in a divorce by compensation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خُلْع<\/bdi>). God knows best. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N115\">[N115]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Nor is it lawful for husbands to “constrain” their wives, retaining them without wishing to fulfil their marital rights, “so as to take away part of what you gave them,” meaning the bridal gift ( الصداق ), by harming them until they surrender it in return for release. Ibn ʿAbbās says that this, too, concerns husbands who keep their wives while mistreating them until they offer the bridal gift to free themselves. “Unless they commit a manifest indecency” ( فَاحِشَةٍ مُبَيِّنَةٍ ), such as rebellious misconduct ( النشوز ), abusive behaviour or unchastity, in which case, on the Mālikī view Ibn ʿAjība records, a husband may constrain her until she redeems herself by a return of the bridal gift in a divorce by compensation ( خُلْع ). God knows best. [N115]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The self requires nourishment as well as spiritual training","html":"<p>The seeker (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المريد<\/bdi>) must not constrict his own self (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النفس<\/bdi>) so harshly that he brings it to destruction. The self is like a working animal: feed it and make use of its service. The Prophet is reported as saying, “Let none of you be like the traveler whose mount collapses: he neither traverses the land nor preserves his mount.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H017\">[H017]<\/a> Some people hear that one who constrains his self will inherit knowledge from it, and therefore impose such severe hardship upon it that they seek to seize that knowledge by force. The right course is only to prevent desires exceeding what is required for the body’s continued existence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قيام البنية<\/bdi>). If the self commits a manifest indecency, growing excessively rebellious, it may be restrained, but never to a degree that leads to destruction. All this benefits the disciple only if his ownership and discipline of the self are established by a sound contract with a complete shaykh (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشيخ الكامل<\/bdi>); without that, the toil is vain, like a person seeking to see another’s wife or riding animal. [Textual note: Cairo prints يرى, “see.” The point of this analogy is difficult in that wording; “possess” is not what the printed verb says, and no alternative reading is independently attested.] And God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The seeker ( المريد ) must not constrict his own self ( النفس ) so harshly that he brings it to destruction. The self is like a working animal: feed it and make use of its service. The Prophet is reported as saying, “Let none of you be like the traveler whose mount collapses: he neither traverses the land nor preserves his mount.” [H017] Some people hear that one who constrains his self will inherit knowledge from it, and therefore impose such severe hardship upon it that they seek to seize that knowledge by force. The right course is only to prevent desires exceeding what is required for the body’s continued existence ( قيام البنية ). If the self commits a manifest indecency, growing excessively rebellious, it may be restrained, but never to a degree that leads to destruction. All this benefits the disciple only if his ownership and discipline of the self are established by a sound contract with a complete shaykh ( الشيخ الكامل ); without that, the toil is vain, like a person seeking to see another’s wife or riding animal. [Textual note: Cairo prints يرى, “see.” The point of this analogy is difficult in that wording; “possess” is not what the printed verb says, and no alternative reading is independently attested.] And God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real then commanded good companionship with women, saying: “Live with them in the recognized manner …”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real then commanded good companionship with women, saying: “Live with them in the recognized manner …”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Good companionship (المعاشرة بالمعروف)","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—says: “Live with women in the recognized good manner” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وَعَاشِرُوهُنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ<\/bdi>), by speaking gently to them and treating them kindly in your conduct; or by adorning yourself for your wife just as she adorns herself for you. Al-Wartajbī comments: In living with your wives, dwell in intimacy (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأنس<\/bdi>), the spirit of love and the joy of ardent affection once you have been singled out for establishment (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التمكين<\/bdi>), uprightness (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاستقامة<\/bdi>) and sainthood (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الولاية<\/bdi>). The companionship of women is proper to the one intimate with God, like the Prophet and all the saints (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأولياء<\/bdi>) and substitutes (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأبدال<\/bdi>) intimate with Him. For the Prophet disclosed the perfection of his intimacy with God and his vision of the beauty of witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المشاهدة<\/bdi>) when he said: “Of your world, three things have been made beloved to me: perfume, women; and the coolness of my eye was placed in prayer.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H018\">[H018]<\/a> Al-Wartajbī further transmits from Dhū al-Nūn that a person intimate with God finds delight in every beautiful thing and pleasing face, every fine voice and agreeable scent. From Ibn al-Mubārak he gives: “Good companionship is that which causes no remorse, either immediately or in the future.” Abū Ḥafṣ says: “Living together in the recognized good manner means showing good character towards one’s family even in what displeases one.” Here ends his report. “If you dislike them,” bear patiently: “It may be that you dislike something and God places much good in it,” perhaps a righteous child or a good outcome for your religion. Ibn ʿUmar says: A man asks God to choose what is best for him, and God chooses; he is then displeased with his Lord, but before long he sees the outcome and discovers that what was chosen was best for him. Here ends his statement. A story is told that the father of Imām Mālik married a woman, entered to meet her and found her dark-skinned. He remained thoughtful and did not approach her. She asked: “Did you seek your Lord’s guidance (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">استخارة<\/bdi>)?” He replied, “Yes.” She said: “Do you suspect your Lord?” He then consummated the marriage, and she conceived Imām Mālik, the founder of the school. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N116\">[N116]<\/a> The Prophet said, “Let no believing man despise a believing woman: if one trait of hers displeases him, another will please him” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لَا يَفْرَكْ مُؤْمِنٌ مُؤْمِنَةً<\/bdi>...). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H019\">[H019]<\/a> Al-Wartajbī transmits an anonymous saying: “Future outcomes are concealed from you so that you will not rest complacently in what is familiar nor flee everything disliked.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—says: “Live with women in the recognized good manner” ( وَعَاشِرُوهُنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ ), by speaking gently to them and treating them kindly in your conduct; or by adorning yourself for your wife just as she adorns herself for you. Al-Wartajbī comments: In living with your wives, dwell in intimacy ( الأنس ), the spirit of love and the joy of ardent affection once you have been singled out for establishment ( التمكين ), uprightness ( الاستقامة ) and sainthood ( الولاية ). The companionship of women is proper to the one intimate with God, like the Prophet and all the saints ( الأولياء ) and substitutes ( الأبدال ) intimate with Him. For the Prophet disclosed the perfection of his intimacy with God and his vision of the beauty of witnessing ( المشاهدة ) when he said: “Of your world, three things have been made beloved to me: perfume, women; and the coolness of my eye was placed in prayer.” [H018] Al-Wartajbī further transmits from Dhū al-Nūn that a person intimate with God finds delight in every beautiful thing and pleasing face, every fine voice and agreeable scent. From Ibn al-Mubārak he gives: “Good companionship is that which causes no remorse, either immediately or in the future.” Abū Ḥafṣ says: “Living together in the recognized good manner means showing good character towards one’s family even in what displeases one.” Here ends his report. “If you dislike them,” bear patiently: “It may be that you dislike something and God places much good in it,” perhaps a righteous child or a good outcome for your religion. Ibn ʿUmar says: A man asks God to choose what is best for him, and God chooses; he is then displeased with his Lord, but before long he sees the outcome and discovers that what was chosen was best for him. Here ends his statement. A story is told that the father of Imām Mālik married a woman, entered to meet her and found her dark-skinned. He remained thoughtful and did not approach her. She asked: “Did you seek your Lord’s guidance ( استخارة )?” He replied, “Yes.” She said: “Do you suspect your Lord?” He then consummated the marriage, and she conceived Imām Mālik, the founder of the school. [N116] The Prophet said, “Let no believing man despise a believing woman: if one trait of hers displeases him, another will please him” ( لَا يَفْرَكْ مُؤْمِنٌ مُؤْمِنَةً ...). [H019] Al-Wartajbī transmits an anonymous saying: “Future outcomes are concealed from you so that you will not rest complacently in what is familiar nor flee everything disliked.”"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"When the self becomes obedient, reconcile with it","html":"<p>When the self has been purified of its remnants, its virtues perfected, and it submits entirely to its Master, it becomes necessary to treat it with kindness, make peace with it and live with it in the recognized good manner. Its struggle and discipline (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مجاهدة<\/bdi>) are required only while it remains unbelieving; when it has submitted in Islam and become obedient, love and kindness towards it become incumbent. If, while still crooked, you dislike it and struggle to train it until it grows upright, its final state will bring much good; it will return bearing divinely bestowed knowledge (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العلوم اللدنية<\/bdi>), and you will behold lordly mysteries within it. Al-Wartajbī says: Every command of God which runs contrary to the self is an examination and trial. The self dislikes servanthood (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العبودية<\/bdi>); but if you impose upon it God’s rights through spiritual training (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرياضة<\/bdi>) and struggle (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المجاهدة<\/bdi>), until it grows upright in His service, the first thing to dawn upon the heart is the lights of the gardens of nearness and witnessing. God says, “And forbade the self its caprice: the Garden shall be its dwelling” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وَنَهَى النَّفْسَ عَنِ الْهَوَى فَإِنَّ الْجَنَّةَ هِيَ الْمَأْوَى<\/bdi>) [Q 79:40–41]. In the inward depths of the darkness of struggling, the knowers behold suns of spiritual combat and moons of unveiling<a class=\"note-ref\" href=\"#/note/XV-N001\">[XV-N001]<\/a> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المكاشفات<\/bdi>). Here ends the substance of his remarks.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"When the self has been purified of its remnants, its virtues perfected, and it submits entirely to its Master, it becomes necessary to treat it with kindness, make peace with it and live with it in the recognized good manner. Its struggle and discipline ( مجاهدة ) are required only while it remains unbelieving; when it has submitted in Islam and become obedient, love and kindness towards it become incumbent. If, while still crooked, you dislike it and struggle to train it until it grows upright, its final state will bring much good; it will return bearing divinely bestowed knowledge ( العلوم اللدنية ), and you will behold lordly mysteries within it. Al-Wartajbī says: Every command of God which runs contrary to the self is an examination and trial. The self dislikes servanthood ( العبودية ); but if you impose upon it God’s rights through spiritual training ( الرياضة ) and struggle ( المجاهدة ), until it grows upright in His service, the first thing to dawn upon the heart is the lights of the gardens of nearness and witnessing. God says, “And forbade the self its caprice: the Garden shall be its dwelling” ( وَنَهَى النَّفْسَ عَنِ الْهَوَى فَإِنَّ الْجَنَّةَ هِيَ الْمَأْوَى ) [Q 79:40–41]. In the inward depths of the darkness of struggling, the knowers behold suns of spiritual combat and moons of unveiling [XV-N001] ( المكاشفات ). Here ends the substance of his remarks."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>If the husband cannot bear the harm he finds in living with his wife and wishes to separate, he must nonetheless pay her the bridal gift, as the Real indicates in the following verse.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"If the husband cannot bear the harm he finds in living with his wife and wishes to separate, he must nonetheless pay her the bridal gift, as the Real indicates in the following verse."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C482.1","page":482,"ar":"الحديث أخرجه أحمد والنسائي والحاكم وغيرهم: بدون لفظ: ثلاث. وقال الحافظ ابن حجر: وليس في شيء من طرقه لفظ «ثلاث»، انظر: الفتح السماوي.","en":"Aḥmad, al-Nasāʾī, al-Ḥākim and others record this hadith without the word “three.” Al-Ḥāfiẓ Ibn Ḥajar says: “The word ‘three’ is not found in any of its transmission routes.” See al-Fatḥ al-samāwī.","anchor":"وجُعلت قرة عيني في الصلاة."}],"english_characters":8765,"arabic_characters":5127},{"id":"q4-20-21","q":"4:20–21","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:20–21 — Return of the bridal gift, the solemn marital covenant, and fidelity to spiritual renunciation","source":"Cairo Arabic edition I:483–484","cairo":"I:483–484","source_page_first":483,"source_page_last":484,"ar":"وَإِنْ أَرَدْتُمُ اسْتِبْدالَ زَوْجٍ مَكانَ زَوْجٍ وَآتَيْتُمْ إِحْداهُنَّ قِنْطاراً فَلا تَأْخُذُوا مِنْهُ شَيْئاً أَتَأْخُذُونَهُ بُهْتاناً وَإِثْماً مُبِيناً (20) وَكَيْفَ تَأْخُذُونَهُ وَقَدْ أَفْضى بَعْضُكُمْ إِلى بَعْضٍ وَأَخَذْنَ مِنْكُمْ مِيثاقاً غَلِيظاً (21)\nقلت: «بهتانًا» : حال، أو على إسقاط الخافض.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَإِنْ أَرَدْتُمُ أن تبدلوا زوجًا مَكانَ زَوْجٍ أخرى بأن تُطلقوا الأولى وتتزوجوا غيرها، وقد كنتم أعطيتم إِحْداهُنَّ قِنْطاراً أو أقل أو أكثر، فَلا تَأْخُذُوا مِنْهُ شَيْئاً بل أدوه لها كاملاً. ثم وبَّخهم على ما كانوا يفعلون في الجاهلية، فقال: أَتَأْخُذُونَهُ بُهْتاناً وَإِثْماً مُبِيناً، أي: مباهتين وآثمين، أو بالبهتان والإثم الظاهر، والبهتان: الكذب الذي يبهت المكذوب عليه، رُوي أن الرجل كان إذا أراد أن يتزوج امرأة\nجديدة، بهت التي عنده بفاحشة حتى يلجئها إلى الافتداء منه بما أعطاها ليصرفه في تزوج الجديدة، فَنُهُوا عن ذلك.\nثم استعظم ذلك فقال: وَكَيْفَ تَأْخُذُونَهُ وَقَدْ أَفْضى بَعْضُكُمْ إِلى بَعْضٍ بالمماسة والجماع حتى تقرر الصداق واستحقته بذلك، وقد أَخَذْنَ مِنْكُمْ مِيثاقاً غَلِيظاً وهو حسن الصحبة، أو الإمساك بالمعروف والتسريح بالإحسان، أو تمكينها نفسها منه، فإنها ما مكنته إلا لوفاء العهد في الصداق ودوام العشرة. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: إذا كان العبد مشتغلاً بجمع دنياه، عاكفًا على حظوظه وهواه، ثم استبدل مكان ذلك الانقطاع إلى مولاه والاشتغال بذكر الله، حتى أفضى إلى شهود أنوار قدسه وسناه، فلا ينبغي أن يرجع إلى شيء خرج عنه لله.\nولا يلتفت إلى ما ترك من أمر دنياه، فإن الرجوع في الشيء من شيم اللئام وليس من شأن الكرام، وتأمل ما قاله الشاعر:\nإذا انْصَرَفَتْ نفسي عن الشيء لم تكن ... إليه بوجهِ آخرَ الدهرِ تُقبِلُ\nوكيف تأخذُ ما خرجت عنه لله، وقد أفضيت إلى شهود أنوار جماله وسُكْنَى حماه، فاتحد عندك كل الوجود، وكل شيء عن عين بصيرتك مفقود، بعد أن أخذ عليك مواثيق العهود، ألا ترجع إلى ما كان يقطعك عن حضرة الشهود، وبالله التوفيق. وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم شرع يتكلم على ما يحرم من النساء، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِنْ أَرَدْتُمُ اسْتِبْدالَ زَوْجٍ مَكانَ زَوْجٍ وَآتَيْتُمْ إِحْداهُنَّ قِنْطاراً فَلا تَأْخُذُوا مِنْهُ شَيْئاً أَتَأْخُذُونَهُ بُهْتاناً وَإِثْماً مُبِيناً (20) وَكَيْفَ تَأْخُذُونَهُ وَقَدْ أَفْضى بَعْضُكُمْ إِلى بَعْضٍ وَأَخَذْنَ مِنْكُمْ مِيثاقاً غَلِيظاً (21)","quran_en":"20. If you wish to exchange one wife for another, even if you have given one of them a vast sum, take back none of it. Would you take it by slander and manifest sin? 21. How could you take it when you have drawn intimately near to one another, and they have taken from you a solemn covenant?","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Buhtānan (بُهْتَانًا) as a circumstantial qualifier or an omitted prepositional construction","html":"<p>I say: “By slander” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بُهْتَانًا<\/bdi>) is accusative as a circumstantial qualifier (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال<\/bdi>), or because a preposition has been omitted (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">على إسقاط الخافض<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “By slander” ( بُهْتَانًا ) is accusative as a circumstantial qualifier ( حال ), or because a preposition has been omitted ( على إسقاط الخافض )."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The bridal payment cannot be recaptured by an invented accusation","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—says: “If you wish to exchange one wife in place of another,” divorcing the first to marry someone else, and you have given one of your wives a <i>qinṭār<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قِنْطَارًا<\/bdi>)—a great quantity of property—or less or more, “do not take any part of it back”; pay it to her in full. He then rebukes practices of the age of ignorance: “Would you take it by slander and manifest sin?”—that is, acting as slanderers and sinners, or using open calumny and wrongdoing. <i>Buhtān<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بُهْتَان<\/bdi>) is a lie that astonishes or confounds the falsely accused. It is related that when a man wanted to marry a new wife he would accuse his existing wife falsely of indecency, forcing her to pay him compensation from the bridal gift he had given her, which he would use to finance the new marriage. The Qurʾān forbade this. The divine address magnifies the wrong: “How could you take it, when you have come intimately into one another’s presence?” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وَقَدْ أَفْضَى بَعْضُكُمْ إِلَى بَعْضٍ<\/bdi>), by touching and marital intercourse, through which the bridal gift is confirmed and the wife’s entitlement completed. “And they have taken from you a solemn covenant” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مِيثَاقًا غَلِيظًا<\/bdi>): this may mean the duty of good companionship; or retaining the wife with recognized kindness and separating with graciousness; or her giving herself to him, which she did only trusting in the fulfilment of the promised bridal gift and continued companionship. And God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—says: “If you wish to exchange one wife in place of another,” divorcing the first to marry someone else, and you have given one of your wives a qinṭār ( قِنْطَارًا )—a great quantity of property—or less or more, “do not take any part of it back”; pay it to her in full. He then rebukes practices of the age of ignorance: “Would you take it by slander and manifest sin?”—that is, acting as slanderers and sinners, or using open calumny and wrongdoing. Buhtān ( بُهْتَان ) is a lie that astonishes or confounds the falsely accused. It is related that when a man wanted to marry a new wife he would accuse his existing wife falsely of indecency, forcing her to pay him compensation from the bridal gift he had given her, which he would use to finance the new marriage. The Qurʾān forbade this. The divine address magnifies the wrong: “How could you take it, when you have come intimately into one another’s presence?” ( وَقَدْ أَفْضَى بَعْضُكُمْ إِلَى بَعْضٍ ), by touching and marital intercourse, through which the bridal gift is confirmed and the wife’s entitlement completed. “And they have taken from you a solemn covenant” ( مِيثَاقًا غَلِيظًا ): this may mean the duty of good companionship; or retaining the wife with recognized kindness and separating with graciousness; or her giving herself to him, which she did only trusting in the fulfilment of the promised bridal gift and continued companionship. And God knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Do not reclaim what you relinquished for God","html":"<p>If a servant was formerly occupied gathering worldly possessions and immersed in his own shares and desires, then exchanged all that for withdrawal to his Master and remembrance of God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ذكر الله<\/bdi>), until he came to witness the lights of divine sanctity and radiance, he should not return to anything he relinquished for God. He should not glance back towards worldly matters he abandoned: returning to what one has renounced is a mark of the ignoble, not the generous. Consider the poet’s words:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">When my soul has turned away from a thing,<br/>It never turns its face back toward that thing, throughout time.<\/blockquote><p>How could you seize again what you gave up for God after attaining the witnessing of the lights of His Beauty and dwelling in His protected precinct? All existence has become unified for you in that vision, and everything has vanished from the sight of your inner eye, after He took solemn covenants from you never to return to what once barred you from the Presence of witnessing (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حضرة الشهود<\/bdi>). God grants success and guides to the straight way.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"If a servant was formerly occupied gathering worldly possessions and immersed in his own shares and desires, then exchanged all that for withdrawal to his Master and remembrance of God ( ذكر الله ), until he came to witness the lights of divine sanctity and radiance, he should not return to anything he relinquished for God. He should not glance back towards worldly matters he abandoned: returning to what one has renounced is a mark of the ignoble, not the generous. Consider the poet’s words: When my soul has turned away from a thing, It never turns its face back toward that thing, throughout time. How could you seize again what you gave up for God after attaining the witnessing of the lights of His Beauty and dwelling in His protected precinct? All existence has become unified for you in that vision, and everything has vanished from the sight of your inner eye, after He took solemn covenants from you never to return to what once barred you from the Presence of witnessing ( حضرة الشهود ). God grants success and guides to the straight way."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He now begins explaining which women are prohibited in marriage.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He now begins explaining which women are prohibited in marriage."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2728,"arabic_characters":1844},{"id":"q4-22","q":"4:22","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:22 — Marriage to a father’s former wife and the boundaries of imitating a shaykh","source":"Cairo Arabic edition I:484–485","cairo":"I:484–485","source_page_first":484,"source_page_last":485,"ar":"وَلا تَنْكِحُوا ما نَكَحَ آباؤُكُمْ مِنَ النِّساءِ إِلاَّ مَا قَدْ سَلَفَ إِنَّهُ كانَ فاحِشَةً وَمَقْتاً وَساءَ سَبِيلاً (22)\nقلت: أوقع «ما» على ما يعقل لقلة عقل النساء، كما تقدم (١) ، أو مصدرية، والاستثناء منقطع أو متصل على وجه المبالغة في التحريم، أي لا تنكحوا ما نكح آباؤكم إِلاَّ مَا قَدْ سَلَفَ لآبائكم إن قدرتم عليه، فهو كقول الشاعر:\nلاَ عَيْبَ فِيهِمْ غَيْرَ أَنَّ سُيُوفَهُمْ ... بِهِنَّ فُلُولٌ مِنْ قِرَاعِ الْكَتَائِبِ (٢)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ولا تتزوجوا ما تزوج به آباؤُكُمْ مِنَ النِّساءِ بالعقد في الحرائر والوطء في الإماء، إِلَّا مَا قَدْ سَلَفَ فإن الله قد عفا عنكم بعد فسخه وردَّه، إِنَّهُ كانَ فاحِشَةً عظيمة عند الله،\nما أحله لأحد من الأمم قبلكم، وَمَقْتاً أي: ممقوتًا فاعله عند الله وعند ذوي المروءات من عباد الله، وكان يسمى ولد الرجل من امرأة أبيه مَقيتًا ومقتيًا. وَساءَ سَبِيلًا، وبئس طريقًا لمن يريد أن يسلكه بعد التحريم.\nفالمراد بالنكاح في الآية: العقد، فعلى هذا لا تحرم المرأة على الولد إذا زنا بها أبوه على المشهور، قال في الرسالة:\nولا يحرم بالزنا حلال. هـ.\nالإشارة: ما جرى في آباء البشرية يجري في آباء الروحانية من طريق الأدب لا من طريق الشرع، فلا ينبغي للمريد أن يتزوج بامرأة شيخه، مات عنها أو طلقها، فإن ذلك قبيح ومقت عند أرباب الأدب، وأما بنت الشيخ فإن قدر على القيام بتعظيمها فلا بأس، وقد تزوج سيدنا علي- كرّم الله وجهه- بنت سيدنا رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم، لكن السلامة في الترك أكثر.\nوهنا إشارة أخرى أرق، وهي أن يشير بالنساء إلى الأحوال، فلا ينبغي للفقير أن يتعاطى أحوال الشيخ، ويفعل مثله. فإن الشيخ في مقام وهو في مقام، فإذا رجع الشيخ إلى الأسباب وتعاطى العلويات، فلا يقتدى به. إلا أن يدرك مقامه، وكان شيخ شيخنا يقول: (لا تقتدوا بالأشياخ في أفعالهم، وإنما اقتدوا بهم في أقوالهم، فإن أقوالهم لكم ولهم، وأفعالهم خاصة بهم) . إِلاَّ مَا قَدْ سَلَفَ لهم من الأحوال في حال سيرهم، فخذوها وسيروا من حيث ساروا، حتى تدركوا ما أدركوا، وافعلوا ما شئتم. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر بقية المحرمات، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلا تَنْكِحُوا ما نَكَحَ آباؤُكُمْ مِنَ النِّساءِ إِلاَّ مَا قَدْ سَلَفَ إِنَّهُ كانَ فاحِشَةً وَمَقْتاً وَساءَ سَبِيلاً (22)","quran_en":"22. Do not marry women whom your fathers married, except for what has already passed. It was a vile indecency, an abomination, and an evil path.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Mā for persons; the exceptional phrase illā mā qad salaf","html":"<p>I say: “What” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مَا<\/bdi>) is applied here to rational beings, and Ibn ʿAjība again explains it—following his earlier discussion—as owing to women’s alleged deficient intellect; or it may be a particle introducing a verbal noun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدرية<\/bdi>). The exception (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاستثناء<\/bdi>) may be disconnected (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">منقطع<\/bdi>), or connected by way of rhetorical exaggeration: “Do not marry those your fathers married, except whatever marriages of theirs lie in the past, if you could do so”; compare the poet:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">They have no defect—except that their swords<br/>Are nicked from striking ranks of fighters.<\/blockquote><p>Here a supposed exception serves to intensify the prohibition rather than remove it. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N121\">[N121]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “What” ( مَا ) is applied here to rational beings, and Ibn ʿAjība again explains it—following his earlier discussion—as owing to women’s alleged deficient intellect; or it may be a particle introducing a verbal noun ( مصدرية ). The exception ( الاستثناء ) may be disconnected ( منقطع ), or connected by way of rhetorical exaggeration: “Do not marry those your fathers married, except whatever marriages of theirs lie in the past, if you could do so”; compare the poet: They have no defect—except that their swords Are nicked from striking ranks of fighters. Here a supposed exception serves to intensify the prohibition rather than remove it. [N121]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"A father’s wife is prohibited to the son","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—says: Do not marry any woman whom your fathers have married, whether by valid contract (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العقد<\/bdi>) where free women are concerned or by sexual access (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الوطء<\/bdi>) in the historical case of women held in bondage, “except what has already passed,” which God has forgiven you after it was dissolved and annulled. “It was an indecency” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فَاحِشَةً<\/bdi>), exceedingly grave before God, and not permitted, he says, to any previous community; “and an abomination” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مَقْتًا<\/bdi>), whose perpetrator is detested before God and among people of honour. A man born from a union with his father’s wife was known as <i>maqīt<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مَقِيت<\/bdi>) or <i>maqtī<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مَقْتِي<\/bdi>). “And an evil path” indeed for anyone choosing it after the prohibition. On this reading “marriage” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النِّكَاح<\/bdi>) means the contract. It follows, according to the predominant doctrine Ibn ʿAjība reports, that if the father committed illicit intercourse with a woman this does not by itself render her forbidden in marriage to the son. As the <i>Risāla<\/i> says, “An unlawful act does not render lawful [relations] forbidden” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ولا يحرم بالزنا حلال<\/bdi>). Here ends his report. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N122\">[N122]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—says: Do not marry any woman whom your fathers have married, whether by valid contract ( العقد ) where free women are concerned or by sexual access ( الوطء ) in the historical case of women held in bondage, “except what has already passed,” which God has forgiven you after it was dissolved and annulled. “It was an indecency” ( فَاحِشَةً ), exceedingly grave before God, and not permitted, he says, to any previous community; “and an abomination” ( مَقْتًا ), whose perpetrator is detested before God and among people of honour. A man born from a union with his father’s wife was known as maqīt ( مَقِيت ) or maqtī ( مَقْتِي ). “And an evil path” indeed for anyone choosing it after the prohibition. On this reading “marriage” ( النِّكَاح ) means the contract. It follows, according to the predominant doctrine Ibn ʿAjība reports, that if the father committed illicit intercourse with a woman this does not by itself render her forbidden in marriage to the son. As the Risāla says, “An unlawful act does not render lawful [relations] forbidden” ( ولا يحرم بالزنا حلال ). Here ends his report. [N122]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The etiquette of the shaykh is not identical with a legal prohibition","html":"<p>What holds of fathers by bodily descent has a parallel among spiritual fathers (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">آباء الروحانية<\/bdi>), by way of spiritual etiquette (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأدب<\/bdi>), <strong>not by way of the revealed law<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لا من طريق الشرع<\/bdi>). A disciple should not marry the widow or former wife of his shaykh; such conduct is regarded as offensive and blameworthy by those concerned with proper spiritual etiquette. With the shaykh’s daughter there is no objection, provided he can fulfil the duty of honouring her. Our master ʿAlī married the daughter of the Messenger of God, yet safety is more likely in refraining. A subtler indication reads “women” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النساء<\/bdi>) as spiritual states (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأحوال<\/bdi>). The poor seeker (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفقير<\/bdi>) should not claim or imitate his shaykh’s states as though those states were his own: the shaykh occupies one station and he another. If a shaykh returns to ordinary means (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأسباب<\/bdi>), or engages with elevated matters, the disciple should not imitate him until he has reached a comparable station. Our shaykh’s shaykh used to say: “Do not imitate the shaykhs in their acts; imitate them in their words. Their words are for you and for them, but their acts are particular to them.” “Except what is past,” meaning the states the masters passed through during their own spiritual journeying (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السير<\/bdi>): take up those stages and journey from where they began until you have reached what they reached; then do as you wish. And God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"What holds of fathers by bodily descent has a parallel among spiritual fathers ( آباء الروحانية ), by way of spiritual etiquette ( الأدب ), not by way of the revealed law ( لا من طريق الشرع ). A disciple should not marry the widow or former wife of his shaykh; such conduct is regarded as offensive and blameworthy by those concerned with proper spiritual etiquette. With the shaykh’s daughter there is no objection, provided he can fulfil the duty of honouring her. Our master ʿAlī married the daughter of the Messenger of God, yet safety is more likely in refraining. A subtler indication reads “women” ( النساء ) as spiritual states ( الأحوال ). The poor seeker ( الفقير ) should not claim or imitate his shaykh’s states as though those states were his own: the shaykh occupies one station and he another. If a shaykh returns to ordinary means ( الأسباب ), or engages with elevated matters, the disciple should not imitate him until he has reached a comparable station. Our shaykh’s shaykh used to say: “Do not imitate the shaykhs in their acts; imitate them in their words. Their words are for you and for them, but their acts are particular to them.” “Except what is past,” meaning the states the masters passed through during their own spiritual journeying ( السير ): take up those stages and journey from where they began until you have reached what they reached; then do as you wish. And God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then mentioned the remaining prohibited relationships.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then mentioned the remaining prohibited relationships."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C484.1","page":484,"ar":"راجع: تفسير قوله تعالى: «وإن خفتم ألا تقسطوا في اليتامى..»، الآية (٣) من هذه السورة.","en":"See the commentary on “If you fear that you will not deal justly with the orphans…”—verse 3 of this sūrah.","anchor":"لقلة عقل النساء، كما تقدم"},{"id":"C484.2","page":484,"ar":"البيت للنابغة الذبياني.","en":"The verse is by al-Nābigha al-Dhubyānī.","anchor":"فُلُولٌ مِنْ قِرَاعِ الْكَتَائِبِ"}],"english_characters":3255,"arabic_characters":1825},{"id":"q4-23","q":"4:23","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:23 — Prohibited degrees of kinship, fosterage and affinity","source":"Cairo Arabic edition I:485–486","cairo":"I:485","source_page_first":485,"source_page_last":485,"ar":"حُرِّمَتْ عَلَيْكُمْ أُمَّهاتُكُمْ وَبَناتُكُمْ وَأَخَواتُكُمْ وَعَمَّاتُكُمْ وَخالاتُكُمْ وَبَناتُ الْأَخِ وَبَناتُ الْأُخْتِ وَأُمَّهاتُكُمُ اللاَّتِي أَرْضَعْنَكُمْ وَأَخَواتُكُمْ مِنَ الرَّضاعَةِ وَأُمَّهاتُ نِسائِكُمْ وَرَبائِبُكُمُ اللاَّتِي فِي حُجُورِكُمْ مِنْ نِسائِكُمُ اللاَّتِي دَخَلْتُمْ بِهِنَّ فَإِنْ لَمْ تَكُونُوا دَخَلْتُمْ بِهِنَّ فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ وَحَلائِلُ أَبْنائِكُمُ الَّذِينَ مِنْ أَصْلابِكُمْ وَأَنْ تَجْمَعُوا بَيْنَ الْأُخْتَيْنِ إِلاَّ مَا قَدْ سَلَفَ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ غَفُوراً رَحِيماً (23)","quran_ar":"حُرِّمَتْ عَلَيْكُمْ أُمَّهاتُكُمْ وَبَناتُكُمْ وَأَخَواتُكُمْ وَعَمَّاتُكُمْ وَخالاتُكُمْ وَبَناتُ الْأَخِ وَبَناتُ الْأُخْتِ وَأُمَّهاتُكُمُ اللاَّتِي أَرْضَعْنَكُمْ وَأَخَواتُكُمْ مِنَ الرَّضاعَةِ وَأُمَّهاتُ نِسائِكُمْ وَرَبائِبُكُمُ اللاَّتِي فِي حُجُورِكُمْ مِنْ نِسائِكُمُ اللاَّتِي دَخَلْتُمْ بِهِنَّ فَإِنْ لَمْ تَكُونُوا دَخَلْتُمْ بِهِنَّ فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ وَحَلائِلُ أَبْنائِكُمُ الَّذِينَ مِنْ أَصْلابِكُمْ وَأَنْ تَجْمَعُوا بَيْنَ الْأُخْتَيْنِ إِلاَّ مَا قَدْ سَلَفَ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ غَفُوراً رَحِيماً (23)","quran_en":"23. Forbidden to you are your mothers and your daughters; your sisters; your paternal aunts and maternal aunts; your brothers’ daughters and sisters’ daughters; your mothers who suckled you and your sisters through suckling; your wives’ mothers; your stepdaughters in your care born of wives with whom you have consummated marriage—but if you have not consummated marriage with them, there is no blame upon you; and the wives of sons who come from your own loins; and that you unite two sisters in marriage, except what has already passed. Truly God is Forgiving, Merciful.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Kitāba Allāhi ʿalaykum as the confirming verbal noun","html":"<p>I say: “God’s ordinance upon you” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كِتَابَ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ<\/bdi>) is an emphatic verbal noun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدر مؤكد<\/bdi>): “God has written this prescription upon you as an ordinance” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كتب الله ذلك كتابًا<\/bdi>); or it may be a construction of direct exhortation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإغراء<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “God’s ordinance upon you” ( كِتَابَ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ ) is an emphatic verbal noun ( مصدر مؤكد ): “God has written this prescription upon you as an ordinance” ( كتب الله ذلك كتابًا ); or it may be a construction of direct exhortation ( الإغراء )."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Women prohibited through descent","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His glory—says: Several classes of women are prohibited to you in marriage, whether by blood relationship (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النسب<\/bdi>), suckling (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرضاع<\/bdi>), or marriage affinity (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المصاهرة<\/bdi>). Those prohibited by descent include “your mothers,” meaning one’s own mother and the maternal and paternal grandmothers however high the line ascends; “your daughters,” meaning one’s daughter, son’s daughter and daughter’s daughter however far the line descends; “your sisters,” including full sisters, sisters by the father and sisters by the mother; “your paternal aunts,” including the father’s sisters and grandfathers’ sisters of every higher generation, full or half-sisters; “your maternal aunts,” the mother’s or grandmothers’ sisters of every higher generation, full or half-sisters; “your brother’s daughters,” daughters of full or paternal brothers and of their descendants; and “your sisters’ daughters,” including daughters and further descendants of full sisters and maternal or paternal half-sisters. The governing principle is that a man cannot marry his ancestors however high, his descendants however low, the descendants of his own parents however low, or the first generation of descendants of any ancestor higher than his parents [i.e. his aunts].<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His glory—says: Several classes of women are prohibited to you in marriage, whether by blood relationship ( النسب ), suckling ( الرضاع ), or marriage affinity ( المصاهرة ). Those prohibited by descent include “your mothers,” meaning one’s own mother and the maternal and paternal grandmothers however high the line ascends; “your daughters,” meaning one’s daughter, son’s daughter and daughter’s daughter however far the line descends; “your sisters,” including full sisters, sisters by the father and sisters by the mother; “your paternal aunts,” including the father’s sisters and grandfathers’ sisters of every higher generation, full or half-sisters; “your maternal aunts,” the mother’s or grandmothers’ sisters of every higher generation, full or half-sisters; “your brother’s daughters,” daughters of full or paternal brothers and of their descendants; and “your sisters’ daughters,” including daughters and further descendants of full sisters and maternal or paternal half-sisters. The governing principle is that a man cannot marry his ancestors however high, his descendants however low, the descendants of his own parents however low, or the first generation of descendants of any ancestor higher than his parents [i.e. his aunts]."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Prohibition arising through suckling","html":"<p>He then lists what is prohibited by suckling: “Your mothers who suckled you and your sisters through suckling.” God names two classes in the verse, while the Sunna establishes that every category prohibited by blood is likewise prohibited by suckling. The Prophet said: “What is prohibited through suckling is what is prohibited through lineage” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يَحْرُمُ مِنَ الرَّضَاعِ مَا يَحْرُمُ مِنَ النَّسَبِ<\/bdi>). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H020\">[H020]<\/a> Accordingly, the seven categories are: foster-mother, foster-daughter, foster-sister, foster-paternal aunt, foster-maternal aunt, foster-niece through a brother and foster-niece through a sister.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then lists what is prohibited by suckling: “Your mothers who suckled you and your sisters through suckling.” God names two classes in the verse, while the Sunna establishes that every category prohibited by blood is likewise prohibited by suckling. The Prophet said: “What is prohibited through suckling is what is prohibited through lineage” ( يَحْرُمُ مِنَ الرَّضَاعِ مَا يَحْرُمُ مِنَ النَّسَبِ ). [H020] Accordingly, the seven categories are: foster-mother, foster-daughter, foster-sister, foster-paternal aunt, foster-maternal aunt, foster-niece through a brother and foster-niece through a sister."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Affinity: mothers-in-law, stepdaughters and daughters-in-law","html":"<p>He then speaks of the women forbidden by marriage affinity: “Your wives’ mothers.” The prohibition on a father’s wife has been mentioned, and that of a son’s wife follows. “Your stepdaughters who are in your care, from wives with whom you have consummated marriage”: the mention of being “in your care” is not a restrictive condition on the majority interpretation, but corresponds to what most often occurs. The daughter is prohibited whether she grew up in his household or not. ʿAlī is reported to have permitted marrying such a stepdaughter if she had not been brought up under the husband’s care. The phrase “with whom you have consummated marriage” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اللَّاتِي دَخَلْتُمْ بِهِنَّ<\/bdi>) is, however, operative by consensus as reported here: if a man merely contracts marriage with a woman but does not consummate it, he may divorce her and marry her daughter. Hence the words: “If you have not consummated marriage with them, there is no blame upon you” in marrying the daughters. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N124\">[N124]<\/a> “And the wives of your sons,” that is, a woman with whom a son has contracted a marriage and to whom he thereby becomes lawfully married. She becomes prohibited to the father by the contract alone. In sum, a father’s wife, a son’s wife and a wife’s mother become prohibited by the contract, whereas the wife’s daughter becomes prohibited only upon consummation with her mother. Contracting marriage with the daughter prohibits her mother; consummation with the mother prohibits the daughter. “Those [sons] who come from your loins” excludes a woman divorced by an adopted son from this particular prohibition, as in the account of Zayd and the Messenger of God.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then speaks of the women forbidden by marriage affinity: “Your wives’ mothers.” The prohibition on a father’s wife has been mentioned, and that of a son’s wife follows. “Your stepdaughters who are in your care, from wives with whom you have consummated marriage”: the mention of being “in your care” is not a restrictive condition on the majority interpretation, but corresponds to what most often occurs. The daughter is prohibited whether she grew up in his household or not. ʿAlī is reported to have permitted marrying such a stepdaughter if she had not been brought up under the husband’s care. The phrase “with whom you have consummated marriage” ( اللَّاتِي دَخَلْتُمْ بِهِنَّ ) is, however, operative by consensus as reported here: if a man merely contracts marriage with a woman but does not consummate it, he may divorce her and marry her daughter. Hence the words: “If you have not consummated marriage with them, there is no blame upon you” in marrying the daughters. [N124] “And the wives of your sons,” that is, a woman with whom a son has contracted a marriage and to whom he thereby becomes lawfully married. She becomes prohibited to the father by the contract alone. In sum, a father’s wife, a son’s wife and a wife’s mother become prohibited by the contract, whereas the wife’s daughter becomes prohibited only upon consummation with her mother. Contracting marriage with the daughter prohibits her mother; consummation with the mother prohibits the daughter. “Those [sons] who come from your loins” excludes a woman divorced by an adopted son from this particular prohibition, as in the account of Zayd and the Messenger of God."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Combining two sisters","html":"<p>“And [it is forbidden] to join two sisters” in marriage, whether full sisters or paternal or maternal half-sisters. This applies to marriage. In the historical institution of ownership of enslaved women without intercourse, no such objection was raised; regarding intercourse with two sisters under that institution, Mālik, al-Shāfiʿī and Abū Ḥanīfa forbade it, while Ibn ʿAjība reports the Ẓāhirīs as permitting it. “Except what has already passed,” meaning what happened in the age of ignorance, has been forgiven. “Truly God is Forgiving, Merciful.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N125\">[N125]<\/a> Ibn ʿAbbās says: The Arabs had treated as forbidden all the unions prohibited by the Sacred Law, except a father’s wife and the simultaneous marriage of two sisters; that is why the divine speech specifically says “except what has already passed” in these two cases.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"“And [it is forbidden] to join two sisters” in marriage, whether full sisters or paternal or maternal half-sisters. This applies to marriage. In the historical institution of ownership of enslaved women without intercourse, no such objection was raised; regarding intercourse with two sisters under that institution, Mālik, al-Shāfiʿī and Abū Ḥanīfa forbade it, while Ibn ʿAjība reports the Ẓāhirīs as permitting it. “Except what has already passed,” meaning what happened in the age of ignorance, has been forgiven. “Truly God is Forgiving, Merciful.” [N125] Ibn ʿAbbās says: The Arabs had treated as forbidden all the unions prohibited by the Sacred Law, except a father’s wife and the simultaneous marriage of two sisters; that is why the divine speech specifically says “except what has already passed” in these two cases."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4599,"arabic_characters":528}];
